(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2a.23b521b2b9.js"]={"21":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Viewed from the front, how far does the primary intestinal loop normally rotate in total?","choices":{"A":"About 90 degrees clockwise","B":"About 180 degrees clockwise","C":"About 90 degrees counterclockwise","D":"About 270 degrees counterclockwise","E":"About 360 degrees counterclockwise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The loop rotates about 270 degrees counterclockwise: about 90 degrees during herniation and the remaining 180 degrees during return to the abdomen. Ninety degrees counterclockwise is only the herniation phase, and clockwise rotation is abnormal. A full 360 degrees overshoots normal rotation, which totals 270 degrees counterclockwise: 90 during herniation plus 180 during return. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise during the 4th to 6th weeks, so the left vagus comes to lie anteriorly (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the jejunum and ileum coil during rotation, whereas the large intestine lengthens without coiling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A full 360 degrees overshoots normal rotation, which totals "}},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms the axis of rotation of the primary intestinal loop?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Vitelline duct and yolk stalk","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Left umbilical vein remnant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior mesenteric artery forms the axis about which the primary intestinal loop rotates. The celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery supply the foregut and hindgut, and the vitelline duct connects the loop apex to the yolk sac. The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not the pivot of the midgut loop; the superior mesenteric artery is the central axis. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic limb of the primary loop forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, and part of the ileum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal mesentery twists around the origin of this artery as the caudal limb moves to the right (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not "}},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do the herniated intestinal loops begin to return to the abdominal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Sixth week","C":"Eighth week","D":"Sixteenth week","E":"Tenth week"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Herniated loops begin to return during the 10th week, aided by regression of the mesonephros, slower liver growth, and abdominal cavity expansion. Herniation begins in the 6th week, and the return is finished by about the 12th week. In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiologic herniation does not begin until the 6th week. In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord; retraction into the abdomen starts at the 10th week. By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, since retraction is complete by the 10th to 12th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal jejunum returns first and settles on the left side (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud returns last and then descends into the right iliac fossa (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiol","C":"In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord","D":"By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, si"}},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Which covering surrounds the herniated viscera in an omphalocele?","choices":{"A":"Skin","B":"Dura","C":"Amnion","D":"Chorion","E":"Pleura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In omphalocele, the viscera herniate through an enlarged umbilical ring and are covered by a membrane of amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly, which gives the sac a smooth, spherical look on ultrasound. Skin covers an umbilical hernia, and gastroschisis has no covering membrane. Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system and plays no part in the omphalocele sac, which is amnion and peritoneum with Wharton's jelly. Chorion is not part of the omphalocele sac; the membrane is amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly. Pleura lines the thoracic cavity, whereas the omphalocele membrane is amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the omphalocele sac as peritoneum plus amnion, light gray and shiny (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele occurs in about 2.5 per 10,000 births with about 25% mortality (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system a","D":"Chorion is not part of the omphalocele sac; the membrane is ","E":"Pleura lines the thoracic cavity, whereas the omphalocele me"}},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do midgut loops normally enter the umbilical cord as physiologic herniation?","choices":{"A":"Third week","B":"Fourth week","C":"Twelfth week","D":"Sixth week","E":"Twentieth week"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rapid elongation of the loop and growth of the liver make the abdomen temporarily too small, so loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord during the 6th week. They return beginning in the 10th week. The third week is too early; herniation into the cord is driven by midgut elongation and liver growth beginning in the 6th week. The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begins in the 6th week. By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the abdomen rather than entering the cord. By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because loops normally return by the 10th to 12th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: coiling of the small intestinal loops and formation of the cecum occur while the gut is herniated (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: physiologic herniation persists until about the 10th week, so bowel in the cord base before then is normal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The third week is too early; herniation into the cord is dri","B":"The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begin","C":"By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the","E":"By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because"}},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the abdominal wall defect of gastroschisis usually located?","choices":{"A":"Through the enlarged umbilical ring itself","B":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the right","C":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the left side","D":"In the upper epigastric midline above it","E":"Just above the pubic symphysis in midline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastroschisis occurs lateral to the umbilicus, usually on the right, from abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk. Herniation through the umbilical ring itself is omphalocele. A suprapubic defect suggests bladder exstrophy. Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the right, not the left. Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper epigastric midline defect. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is a lateral fold closure defect, while bladder exstrophy is a caudal fold defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis is increasing in frequency, especially in babies of mothers under 20 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the","D":"Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper"}},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Meckel diverticulum results from persistence of which embryonic structure?","choices":{"A":"Urachal remnant","B":"Allantoic stalk","C":"Vitelline duct","D":"Cloacal membrane","E":"Umbilical vein"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A small persisting portion of the vitelline duct forms an outpocketing of the ileum, Meckel diverticulum. A persisting urachus comes from the allantois and drains urine. The cloacal membrane and umbilical vein are not related to the ileum. The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, not to an ileal outpouching. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: the diverticulum usually causes no symptoms in adults (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, "}},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Along which border of the ileum does Meckel diverticulum arise?","choices":{"A":"Antimesenteric border","B":"Mesenteric border","C":"Duodenojejunal border","D":"Retroperitoneal border","E":"Ileocecal valve border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meckel diverticulum lies on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve. It does not arise from the mesenteric border, the duodenojejunal flexure, or the ileocecal valve itself. The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal on its mesentery; the diverticulum arises on the antimesenteric border, not a retroperitoneal one. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rule of 2s lists 2 feet from the ileocecal valve and 2 inches long (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum is twice as likely in males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal o"}},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"What anomaly results when the omphalomesenteric duct stays patent along its entire length?","choices":{"A":"Vitelline cyst","B":"Urachal sinus tract","C":"Duplication cyst","D":"Omphalocele sac","E":"Vitelline fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A duct that stays patent throughout forms a vitelline, or umbilical, fistula connecting the ileal lumen with the umbilicus, so feces may drain at the umbilicus. A vitelline cyst forms when only the middle part persists between two fibrous cords. A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the bladder, not a patent omphalomesenteric duct reaching the ileum. Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parenchyma, most often near the ileum, unrelated to a patent vitelline duct. An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops through an enlarged umbilical ring, not a patent vitelline duct. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: umbilical drainage of feces points to a patent omphalomesenteric duct, whereas urine drainage points to a patent urachus (Langman Ch. 16). COMLEX: the vitelline duct connects the apex of the primary loop with the yolk sac (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the ","C":"Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parench","D":"An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops throu"}},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment do most atresias and stenoses occur?","choices":{"A":"Jejunum","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Appendix","D":"Duodenum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most atresias and stenoses occur in the duodenum, the fewest occur in the colon, and the jejunum and ileum have equal numbers (about 1 in 1500 births). The appendix is not a usual atresia site. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia is associated with Down syndrome and shows a double bubble on x-ray (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: atresias account for 95% of these defects and stenoses for only 5% (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism most likely causes atresia of the upper duodenum, just distal to the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Lack of recanalization","B":"Vascular accident","C":"Midgut volvulus","D":"Amniotic fluid injury","E":"Persistent vitelline cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Upper duodenal atresias are probably due to failure of the solid duodenal lumen to recanalize. Distal to the duodenum, atresias have been attributed to vascular accidents, which can follow volvulus, malrotation, or abdominal wall defects. Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in gastroschisis but does not cause upper duodenal atresia. A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twisting around it, not upper duodenal atresia. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and distension within the first 1 to 2 days of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: the duodenal lumen is temporarily obliterated by epithelial proliferation before recanalizing (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in ga","E":"A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twis"}},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Misexpression of which gene families supports a non-vascular, differentiation-based cause of intestinal atresia?","choices":{"A":"RET and GDNF family receptor genes","B":"HOX genes and FGF family genes","C":"SHH and patched receptor genes","D":"PAX and SOX transcription factor genes","E":"WNT and BMP signaling pathway genes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Misexpression of HOX genes and of FGF family ligands and receptors disrupts epithelial-mesenchymal signaling and causes gut atresias, showing that faulty gut differentiation, not only vascular accidents, can produce these defects. RET mutations cause aganglionic megacolon. SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for craniocaudal patterning; the notes tie atresia specifically to HOX and FGF misexpression. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET mutations impair neural crest migration and cause Hirschsprung disease (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: in 50% of atresias a region of bowel is lost, and in 20% a fibrous cord remains (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for cra"}},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which germ layer forms the epithelium lining the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Ectoderm","B":"Neural crest","C":"Somatic layer","D":"Paraxial layer","E":"Endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The epithelium of the digestive system and the parenchyma of its derivatives originate in endoderm. Connective tissue, muscle, and peritoneal components come from mesoderm. Ectoderm forms only the caudal anal canal. Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epithelium, which derives from gut tube endoderm. Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is the splanchnic layer, and the epithelium itself is endoderm. Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epithelium, which is endodermal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudal third of the anal canal is ectodermal and is supplied by the inferior rectal artery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: gut differentiation depends on reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal interactions (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epitheli","C":"Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is t","D":"Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epitheli"}},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic tissue forms the smooth muscle of the small intestinal wall?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm","B":"Gut tube endoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Paraxial mesoderm","E":"Somatic body wall mesoderm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscular and connective tissue components of the gut come from mesoderm, specifically the splanchnic (visceral) layer surrounding the endodermal tube. Endoderm forms the epithelium. Somatic and paraxial mesoderm form body wall and somite derivatives. Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the caudal anal canal, not intestinal smooth muscle. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endodermal sonic hedgehog induces HOX gene expression in the surrounding gut mesoderm (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: visceral peritoneum covers the gut while parietal peritoneum lines the body wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the cauda"}},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure is the most distal derivative of the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the transverse colon and flexure","B":"Proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon","C":"Descending colon at the splenic flexure region","D":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve junction","E":"Upper anal canal above the pectinate line"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon with the distal third. The distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, and the upper anal canal are hindgut. The terminal ileum is proximal to the end. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut receives vagal parasympathetic innervation and lies at the L1 vertebral level of its artery (First Aid Gastrointestinal, blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: the caudal limb of the primary loop forms the lower ileum, cecum, appendix, and ascending colon (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has sudden bilious vomiting. An upper GI series shows the small intestinal loops lying in the right abdomen, and a contrast enema places the cecum and colon in the left abdomen. The ileum enters the cecum from the right. Which developmental error best explains this anatomy?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of the primary loop","B":"Complete rotation with failed colonic fixation","C":"Failure of the loops to leave the abdomen","D":"Persistent attachment of the omphalomesenteric duct","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When rotation amounts to only 90 degrees, the colon and cecum return first and settle on the left, and later loops move to the right, giving a left-sided colon. Clockwise (reversed) rotation places the transverse colon behind the duodenum. Failed fixation leaves a mobile colon in its normal place. If loops never left the abdomen there would be no physiologic herniation, and this would not explain a left-sided colon with ileum entering from the right. A persistent omphalomesenteric duct causes a Meckel diverticulum or fistula, not a left-sided colon. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: malrotation leaves the small bowel clumped on the right and the colon on the left, with fibrous Ladd bands (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malrotation). COMLEX: malrotation predisposes to volvulus with compromised blood supply (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"If loops never left the abdomen there would be no physiologi","D":"A persistent omphalomesenteric duct causes a Meckel divertic"}},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl with known intestinal malrotation abruptly develops bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and bloody stools. Heart rate is 190/min, and capillary refill is 4 seconds. Which complication most likely threatens her bowel?","choices":{"A":"Telescoping of ileum into the cecum","B":"Twisting that compromises blood flow","C":"Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment","D":"Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa","E":"Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Malrotation may result in twisting of the intestine (volvulus) and a compromised blood supply, which explains bloody stools and shock. Intussusception, aganglionosis, Meckel ulceration, and meconium ileus cause obstruction or bleeding by other mechanisms and are not the typical malrotation emergency. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut volvulus is more common in infants, whereas sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: in gastroschisis, volvulus with compromised blood supply may kill large regions of bowel (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman undergoes CT for flank pain. The radiologist notes that her transverse colon passes posterior to the duodenum and lies behind the superior mesenteric artery. Which embryologic event best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","B":"Counterclockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","C":"Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon","D":"Arrested descent of the cecal bud from the liver","E":"Return of the colon before the proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reversed rotation occurs when the primary loop rotates 90 degrees clockwise, so the transverse colon passes behind the duodenum and the superior mesenteric artery. Counterclockwise rotation of only 90 degrees produces a left-sided colon. Failed attachment produces a mobile colon. The cecal bud descends from the right upper quadrant, and arrested descent leaves a high cecum, not a colon behind the duodenum and SMA. Colon returning first, before the small bowel, is the pattern of 90-degree incomplete rotation giving a left-sided colon. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after normal rotation, the transverse colon passes in front of the duodenum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the liver before descending (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The cecal bud descends from the right upper quadrant, and ar","E":"Colon returning first, before the small bowel, is the patter"}},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a routine ultrasound at 20 weeks of gestation. It shows a smooth, membrane-bound midline sac containing bowel and liver at the cord insertion. Which associated finding should next be sought?","choices":{"A":"Renal agenesis","B":"Cardiac anomaly","C":"Hip dysplasia","D":"Cleft palate","E":"Clubfoot deformity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is omphalocele, which is associated with cardiac anomalies in about 50%, neural tube defects in about 40%, and chromosomal abnormalities in about 15% of live-born infants. The other anomalies are not the classic associations. Renal agenesis is not a listed omphalocele association; cardiac (50%), neural tube (40%), and chromosomal (15%) defects are. Hip dysplasia is not among omphalocele's associated defects, which are cardiac, neural tube, and chromosomal anomalies. Cleft palate is not a classic omphalocele association; cardiac anomalies are the most common at about 50%. Clubfoot is not among omphalocele's associated defects, which are cardiac, neural tube, and chromosomal anomalies. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: omphalocele is associated with trisomies 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele arises when bowel fails to return from physiologic herniation during weeks 6 to 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Renal agenesis is not a listed omphalocele association; card","C":"Hip dysplasia is not among omphalocele's associated defects,","D":"Cleft palate is not a classic omphalocele association; cardi","E":"Clubfoot is not among omphalocele's associated defects, whic"}},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn boy is delivered to an 18-year-old mother. Thickened, uncovered bowel loops protrude through a small defect just to the right of a normal umbilical cord. No other anomalies are found. Which developmental error most likely caused this defect?","choices":{"A":"Failure of loops to return from the umbilical cord","B":"Persistence of a patent omphalomesenteric duct","C":"Delayed closure of the umbilical ring after birth","D":"Abnormal closure of the body wall around the stalk","E":"Failed recanalization of the solid gut lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastroschisis is most likely due to abnormal closure of the body wall around the connecting stalk. The bowel is exposed to amniotic fluid and may be damaged. Failed return of herniated loops causes omphalocele, and delayed umbilical ring closure causes an umbilical hernia covered by skin. A patent omphalomesenteric duct forms a vitelline fistula draining feces at the umbilicus, not bare loops beside a normal cord. Failed recanalization causes upper duodenal atresia, not a full-thickness abdominal wall defect. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is not commonly associated with chromosomal abnormalities and has a good prognosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis occurs in about 1 per 10,000 births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A patent omphalomesenteric duct forms a vitelline fistula dr","E":"Failed recanalization causes upper duodenal atresia, not a f"}},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman has an early ultrasound at 9 weeks of gestation. Several bowel loops are seen within the base of the umbilical cord. The fetal heart, spine, and limbs appear normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate for this normal-appearing fetus?","choices":{"A":"Omphalocele with amnion covering","B":"Gastroschisis with uncovered loops","C":"Physiologic midgut herniation","D":"Congenital umbilical hernia","E":"Vitelline cyst at the umbilicus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Midgut loops normally herniate into the cord from the 6th week and begin returning in the 10th week, so bowel in the cord at 9 weeks is expected. Omphalocele and gastroschisis are diagnosed later in gestation. A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and pathologic, whereas bowel in the cord at 9 weeks is the normal physiologic herniation. A vitelline cyst is a dilated central duct segment between fibrous cords, not loops of bowel in the cord base. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a congenital umbilical hernia is covered by skin and often closes spontaneously (First Aid Gastrointestinal, congenital umbilical hernia). COMLEX: herniation occurs because liver growth makes the abdomen temporarily too small (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and pathologic","E":"A vitelline cyst is a dilated central duct segment between f"}},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has painless passage of a large amount of maroon blood per rectum. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (reference range: 10.5–13.5 g/dL). A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. What most likely caused his bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Acid secretion by ectopic gastric mucosa","B":"Ischemia from an ileocolic intussusception","C":"Angiodysplasia within the cecal mucosal wall","D":"Hyperplasia of the ileal lymphoid follicles","E":"Erosion by a swallowed coin or button battery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The scan detects heterotopic gastric mucosa in an ileal diverticulum. Acid from this mucosa ulcerates adjacent ileal mucosa, causing bleeding or perforation. Intussusception causes pain, angiodysplasia is a disease of older adults, and lymphoid hyperplasia does not take up pertechnetate. A swallowed foreign body would not concentrate pertechnetate, which localizes to ectopic gastric mucosa in a Meckel diverticulum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pertechnetate scan is called a Meckel scan because it detects heterotopic gastric mucosa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: heterotopic pancreatic tissue in the diverticulum can also cause ulceration (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A swallowed foreign body would not concentrate pertechnetate"}},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has colicky pain, bilious vomiting, and distension. At surgery, a cyst lies between the ileum and the umbilicus, attached at both ends by fibrous cords, and a loop of ileum is wrapped around one cord. Which mechanism caused her obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal ulceration with free perforation","B":"Volvulus of loops on the vitelline ligaments","C":"Telescoping of the ileum into the ascending colon","D":"Aganglionic spasm of a distal colonic segment","E":"Inspissated meconium filling the ileal lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When both ends of the duct become fibrous cords and the middle forms a vitelline cyst, the cords cross the peritoneal cavity, so loops may twist around them, causing obstruction, strangulation, or volvulus. Ulceration is the bleeding mechanism of ectopic mucosa. Telescoping of ileum into colon is intussusception, for which Meckel acts as a lead point, but here the loop is wrapped around a vitelline cord. Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease of the colon and does not involve vitelline cords. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: a vitelline cyst is also called an enterocystoma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Telescoping of ileum into colon is intussusception, for whic","D":"Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease of the colon and d"}},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has right lower quadrant pain. At laparoscopy, the appendix is normal, but an inflamed 5-cm outpouching arises from the ileum about 50 cm from the ileocecal valve. Pathology shows mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis propria in its wall. How is this outpouching best classified?","choices":{"A":"Duplication cyst","B":"False diverticulum","C":"Pulsion pseudodiverticulum","D":"Pancreatic rest","E":"True diverticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A persistent vitelline duct remnant contains all layers of the bowel wall, so it is a true diverticulum. False diverticula contain only mucosa and submucosa. Duplication cysts are separate tubular or cystic structures, most often near the ileum. A pulsion pseudodiverticulum lacks the muscularis propria, which this lesion contains. A pancreatic rest is ectopic tissue within a wall, often inside a Meckel diverticulum, not a full-thickness outpouching itself. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum commonly presents in the first 2 years of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: about 2% of people have a Meckel diverticulum (lecture notes; Langman gives 2% to 4%).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A pulsion pseudodiverticulum lacks the muscularis propria, w","D":"A pancreatic rest is ectopic tissue within a wall, often ins"}},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn girl has bilious vomiting and distension on day 1. Her mother used cocaine during pregnancy. Abdominal x-ray shows several dilated proximal loops and a gasless colon. At surgery, the proximal jejunum ends blindly, separated from distal bowel by a V-shaped mesenteric gap. Which mechanism best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Ischemic necrosis of a fetal intestinal segment","B":"Failure of solid-cord recanalization of the lumen","C":"Traction by a persistent vitelline fibrous cord","D":"Exposure of loops to amniotic fluid through a defect","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees of turning"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Atresias distal to the duodenum have been attributed to vascular accidents that compromise blood flow and cause necrosis and loss of a bowel segment. Vasoconstrictive drugs raise this risk. Failure of recanalization is the mechanism for upper duodenal atresia. A persistent vitelline cord obstructs by loops twisting around it, not by leaving a blind jejunum with a V-shaped mesenteric gap. Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, but no abdominal wall defect is described here. Rotation stopping at 90 degrees gives a left-sided colon and volvulus risk, not a blind-ended jejunum with a mesenteric gap. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35); First Aid Gastrointestinal (intestinal atresia). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal and ileal atresia are associated with cystic fibrosis and gastroschisis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: maternal tobacco smoking is a risk factor for jejunal and ileal atresia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A persistent vitelline cord obstructs by loops twisting arou","D":"Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, but n","E":"Rotation stopping at 90 degrees gives a left-sided colon and"}},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A preterm newborn with low birth weight has bilious vomiting. At surgery, the proximal jejunum ends blindly, and the short distal small intestine is coiled around a thin mesenteric remnant. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Membranous duodenal web","B":"Annular pancreas","C":"Apple peel atresia","D":"Multiple segmental atresias","E":"Midgut duplication cyst"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Apple peel atresia accounts for about 10% of atresias. It occurs in the proximal jejunum, and the shortened distal intestine is coiled around a remnant of the mesentery. Membranous webs and annular pancreas obstruct the duodenum. Multiple atresias are separate segmental gaps, not one short distal bowel coiled helically around a mesenteric remnant. Duplication cysts are separate tubular or cystic structures, most often near the ileum, not a coiled distal bowel after jejunal atresia. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: effects of an atresia depend on how much bowel is damaged and where it lies (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a thin diaphragm separating larger and smaller pieces of bowel accounts for about 20% of atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Multiple atresias are separate segmental gaps, not one short","E":"Duplication cysts are separate tubular or cystic structures,"}},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn boy has several separate ileal atresias found at surgery. The mesenteric vessels are intact, there was no malrotation or volvulus, and an older sister had a similar defect. Which mechanism most likely explains his atresias?","choices":{"A":"Intrauterine volvulus with segment infarction","B":"Failed recanalization of the solid lumen","C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drug exposure","D":"Faulty gene-directed gut differentiation","E":"Amniotic injury through an abdominal wall defect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"New evidence suggests that problems with gut differentiation, including misexpression of developmental genes and growth factor receptors, can cause atresias without a vascular accident. Intact vessels and no volvulus argue against ischemia, and recanalization failure applies to the upper duodenum. Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs act through vascular accidents, which intact vessels here argue against. Amniotic injury requires an abdominal wall defect like gastroschisis, which is not described. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stenoses and multiple atresias each account for about 5% of these defects (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about 33% of intestinal duplications are associated with other defects such as atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs act through vascular acciden","E":"Amniotic injury requires an abdominal wall defect like gastr"}},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the superior mesenteric artery just beyond its origin. Which bowel segment is most likely to be spared from ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Proximal jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Descending colon","D":"Cecum and appendix","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The jejunum, ileum, cecum, and ascending colon are midgut derivatives supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hindgut extends from the distal third of the transverse colon to the anal canal above the pectinate line (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the left third of the transverse colon is a hindgut derivative (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has severe celiac trunk stenosis, but her distal duodenum stays well perfused. Which landmark marks the embryologic boundary between foregut and midgut territory in her duodenum?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Minor duodenal papilla","C":"Duodenojejunal flexure","D":"First-part duodenal bulb","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The foregut includes the duodenum to the level of the liver and ventral pancreatic buds (major papilla), and the midgut begins just distal to the entrance of the bile duct. The duodenum caudal to the major papilla is therefore midgut. The pylorus is proximal to the major papilla and so lies in foregut territory. The minor papilla is not the boundary; the foregut-midgut transition is just distal to the major papilla where the bile duct enters. The duodenojejunal flexure lies well within midgut territory, distal to the boundary at the major papilla. The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum supplied by the celiac trunk, not the boundary. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut extends from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the ampulla of Vater (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the liver, pancreas, and biliary apparatus grow out of the upper duodenal endoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The pylorus is proximal to the major papilla and so lies in ","B":"The minor papilla is not the boundary; the foregut-midgut tr","C":"The duodenojejunal flexure lies well within midgut territory","D":"The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum supplied by the celia"}},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn with holoprosencephaly has a loss-of-function mutation in a secreted signaling gene, and imaging shows gut malformations as well. In the developing gut, which signal from the endoderm patterns the surrounding mesoderm along the craniocaudal axis?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"Bone morphogenetic protein 4","D":"Sonic hedgehog","E":"Fibroblast growth factor 10"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gut endoderm secretes sonic hedgehog, which induces HOX genes in the surrounding mesoderm that then regulate the craniocaudal organization of the gut. A retinoic acid gradient specifies gut regions but is not the endodermal signal to mesoderm. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HOX genes in gut mesoderm, induced by endodermal signaling, regulate craniocaudal organization of the gut and its derivatives (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: an RA gradient causes region-specific transcription factors along the gut tube (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman is at 11 weeks of gestation, and her fetus is undergoing normal midgut return. Where does the cecal bud lie just after it reenters the abdomen?","choices":{"A":"Right upper quadrant","B":"Left upper quadrant","C":"Right lower quadrant","D":"Left lower quadrant","E":"Pelvic cavity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cecal bud is the last part to reenter and lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the right lobe of the liver. It then descends into the right iliac fossa. A left-sided colon results only from incomplete midgut rotation, not from normal return of the midgut. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops, Fig. 15.27). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in about 50% of people the appendix is retrocecal or retrocolic because it develops during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A left-sided colon results only from incomplete midgut rotat"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn has a midline membrane-covered umbilical sac, macroglossia, and hypoglycemia. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Down syndrome","B":"Turner syndrome","C":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","D":"DiGeorge syndrome","E":"Marfan syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Omphalocele is associated with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome and trisomies 13 and 18. Macroglossia and neonatal hypoglycemia point to Beckwith-Wiedemann. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (ventral wall defects); Langman Ch. 15 (Body wall defects). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 15% of live-born infants with omphalocele have chromosomal abnormalities (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: omphalocele reflects failed return of physiologically herniated loops, whereas gastroschisis reflects abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk (lecture notes; Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man asks how common his incidentally found ileal diverticulum is. What fraction of people have one?","choices":{"A":"About 0.1%","B":"About 10%","C":"About 25%","D":"About 40%","E":"About 2%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 2% of people have a persistent proximal vitelline duct forming an ileal diverticulum, matching First Aid's rule of 2s; Langman gives a range of 2% to 4%. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum may contain two types of ectopic epithelium, gastric and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum can present with painless hematochezia or melena (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has bilious vomiting from jejunal atresia. Which finding is expected on her abdominal x-ray?","choices":{"A":"Triple bubble sign","B":"Double bubble sign","C":"String sign","D":"Bird beak sign","E":"Apple core lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jejunal and ileal atresia may show a triple bubble (dilated stomach, duodenum, and proximal jejunum) with a gasless colon. A double bubble suggests duodenal atresia. A string sign reflects the narrowed lumen of Crohn disease with transmural thickening, not neonatal atresia. A bird-beak sign on barium swallow is the classic finding of achalasia at the lower esophageal sphincter. An apple core lesion is a stenosing malignancy seen on barium study, as in colorectal or esophageal cancer. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (intestinal atresia); Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in jejunal and ileal atresia, ischemic necrosis leads to segmental resorption and discontinuous bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: a narrowing with a thin diaphragm is one of the common atresia patterns (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"C":"A string sign reflects the narrowed lumen of Crohn disease w","D":"A bird-beak sign on barium swallow is the classic finding of","E":"An apple core lesion is a stenosing malignancy seen on bariu"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn has gut segments with abnormal regional identity. Which signal specifies gut regions along the craniocaudal axis before epithelial-mesenchymal interactions?","choices":{"A":"Sonic hedgehog gradient","B":"Retinoic acid gradient","C":"BMP4 gradient","D":"FGF10 gradient","E":"Indian hedgehog gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Langman states that regions of the gut tube such as the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum are specified by a retinoic acid gradient that induces region-specific transcription factors. Sonic hedgehog, BMP4, FGF10, and Indian hedgehog act later in reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling rather than setting craniocaudal regional identity. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Summary). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: differentiation of gut derivatives depends on reciprocal endoderm-mesoderm interactions (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the gut tube extends from the oropharyngeal membrane to the cloacal membrane (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has recurrent right-sided abdominal pain. CT shows small bowel loops trapped behind a poorly attached ascending colon. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Paraduodenal hernia","B":"Umbilical hernia","C":"Hiatal hernia","D":"Retrocolic hernia","E":"Inguinal hernia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Incomplete attachment of the ascending colon to the posterior body wall may leave retrocolic pockets, and small intestine trapped behind the ascending colon forms a retrocolic hernia. A paraduodenal hernia traps intestine in the fossa to the left of the ascending duodenum near the duodenojejunal flexure, not behind the ascending colon. A congenital umbilical hernia is a skin-covered defect from delayed closure of the umbilical ring, not entrapment behind the colon. A sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction and cardia into the posterior mediastinum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a mobile ascending colon that fails to attach may twist, causing volvulus of the cecum and colon (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Toldt fascia lies between peritoneal layers where colon attaches to the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"A":"A paraduodenal hernia traps intestine in the fossa to the le","B":"A congenital umbilical hernia is a skin-covered defect from ","C":"A sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction "}}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the cecum and ascending colon?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Splenic artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cecum and ascending colon are midgut derivatives and are supplied by the superior mesenteric artery through its ileocolic and right colic branches. The celiac trunk and splenic artery supply foregut structures, and the internal iliac and median sacral arteries supply pelvic structures rather than midgut colon. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut ends where the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon meet the distal third (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches serve the colonic part of this territory (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Where along the transverse colon does midgut territory end and hindgut territory begin?","choices":{"A":"Junction of its proximal two-thirds and distal third","B":"Junction of its proximal third and distal two-thirds","C":"Midpoint between the two colic flexures","D":"Hepatic flexure at the right colic angle","E":"Splenic flexure at the left colic angle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The midgut extends to the junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal third of the transverse colon; the distal third belongs to the hindgut. The proximal-third split reverses the proportions, the midpoint is not the boundary, the hepatic flexure lies well within midgut territory, and the boundary lies just before the splenic flexure rather than at it. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: this boundary is why the splenic flexure region is a watershed between superior and inferior mesenteric supply (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI blood supply). COMLEX: hindgut derivatives continue caudally to the pectinate line of the anal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following large intestine structures develops from the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Vermiform appendix","C":"Descending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Upper anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut forms the cecum, vermiform appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon, descending colon, rectum, and upper anal canal all come from the hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about half of appendices lie retrocecal or retrocolic because the appendix grows during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which connective tissue layer anchors the ascending and descending colon to the posterior abdominal wall?","choices":{"A":"Scarpa fascia","B":"Camper fascia","C":"Toldt fascia","D":"Denonvilliers fascia","E":"Transversalis fascia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the ascending and descending colon settle against the posterior wall, their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum through Toldt fascia. Scarpa and Camper fascia are layers of the anterior abdominal subcutaneous tissue, Denonvilliers fascia lies between rectum and prostate, and transversalis fascia lines the inner abdominal wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgeons mobilize the colon along the white line of Toldt during colectomy (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the mesentery of the sigmoid colon persists, so this segment stays intraperitoneal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Once their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum, the ascending and descending colon are classified as which type of structure?","choices":{"A":"Primarily retroperitoneal","B":"Intraperitoneal on a mesentery","C":"Infraperitoneal in the pelvis","D":"Extraperitoneal in the wall","E":"Secondarily retroperitoneal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colon first hangs on a dorsal mesentery and becomes secondarily retroperitoneal after fusion with the posterior wall. Primary retroperitoneal organs, such as the kidneys, develop without a mesentery; intraperitoneal describes segments that keep a mesentery, such as the transverse and sigmoid colon; infraperitoneal and extraperitoneal describe pelvic or body wall positions. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum except its first part and the pancreas except its tail are also secondarily retroperitoneal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal structures). COMLEX: the transverse mesocolon fuses with the greater omentum rather than the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"After midgut rotation, which process carries the cecum from the right upper quadrant to the right lower quadrant?","choices":{"A":"Rotation of the stomach","B":"Elongation of the colon","C":"Enlargement of the liver","D":"Regression of the mesonephros","E":"Fusion of the mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cecal bud lies first in the right upper quadrant below the liver, and lengthening of the colon carries the cecum and appendix caudally into the right iliac fossa. Gastric rotation shapes the foregut, liver growth and mesonephric regression influence the earlier return of herniated loops, and mesocolic fusion fixes the colon after it is in place. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cecal bud is the last part of the herniated loop to reenter the abdomen (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a cecum that stays high produces a subhepatic appendix with atypical right upper quadrant pain (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms between the anal and urogenital openings after the cloaca is divided?","choices":{"A":"Genital tubercle","B":"Urogenital fold","C":"Anal column","D":"Perineal body","E":"Labioscrotal swelling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When the cloacal membrane ruptures, the tip of the dividing mesodermal wedge remains between the anal and urogenital openings as the perineal body (Langman-only detail; not in SDL 22 lecture notes). The genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings are external genital primordia, and anal columns are mucosal folds of the upper anal canal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; tip of urorectal septum → perineal body, Figs. 15.36–15.37); SDL 22 lecture notes (urorectal septum context). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the perineal body anchors the perineal muscles and can be torn during vaginal delivery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the allantois enters the anterior cloaca and the hindgut enters the posterior cloaca (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"At the end of which week does the anal membrane (posterior cloacal membrane) break down?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Seventh week","C":"Fifth week","D":"Tenth week","E":"Twelfth week"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anal membrane (the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane) degenerates at the end of the seventh week, opening communication between the anal pit and the hindgut. The fourth and fifth weeks are too early because the cloaca is still being partitioned, and the tenth and twelfth weeks correspond to return of the herniated midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (imperforate anus / anal membrane); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectourethral and rectovaginal fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these defects likely reflect misexpression of genes during epithelial–mesenchymal signaling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"If the urorectal septum fails to extend far enough caudally, how is the hindgut opening displaced?","choices":{"A":"Posterior shift of the hindgut opening","B":"Lateral shift of the hindgut opening","C":"Cranial shift of the hindgut opening","D":"Duplication of the hindgut opening","E":"Anterior shift of the hindgut opening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A small cloaca or a urorectal septum that stops short causes the hindgut opening to shift anteriorly, so it can empty into the urethra, vagina, or perineum. Posterior, lateral, and cranial displacement are not described, and duplication is a separate anomaly unrelated to septal growth. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an anterior opening explains meconium in the urine of a boy with a rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: some variants end in rectal atresia or a fibrous cord to the perineum (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelium lines the anal canal just below the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","B":"Transitional epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the ectodermal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium that continues to the perianal skin. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines ducts and tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line are typically squamous cell carcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: an anal transitional zone lies at the junction of the two epithelial types (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of which vessel?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric artery","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Internal pudendal artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Left colic artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, and supplies the anal canal above the pectinate line. The internal iliac and internal pudendal arteries supply the region below the line, the median sacral artery is a small midline branch of the aorta, and the left colic artery is a separate, more proximal branch. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids above the line drain to the superior rectal vein and portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: sigmoidal branches arise from the same parent vessel between the left colic and terminal branch (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal landmark lies immediately above the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Anal verge","B":"Perianal skin","C":"Intersphincteric groove","D":"Anal columns","E":"Levator ani"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pectinate line, which marks the junction of endodermal and ectodermal parts of the anal canal, lies just below the anal columns. The anal verge and perianal skin lie below it at the outer margin, the intersphincteric groove lies between the sphincters below the line, and the levator ani is pelvic floor muscle rather than a mucosal landmark. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral pain fibers serve the canal above the line, and somatic fibers serve it below (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the cranial two-thirds of the anal canal come from the primitive anorectal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Parasympathetic ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system derive from which embryonic cells?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm cells","B":"Paraxial mesoderm cells","C":"Surface ectoderm cells","D":"Neural tube cells","E":"Neural crest cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enteric parasympathetic ganglion cells come from neural crest cells that migrate from the neural folds into the bowel wall. Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue, paraxial mesoderm forms somites, surface ectoderm forms epidermis, and the neural tube forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural crest derivatives also include adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and Schwann cells (First Aid Embryology, germ layer derivatives). COMLEX: the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway also supports these migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"The RET gene that is mutated in congenital megacolon encodes which type of protein?","choices":{"A":"Ligand-gated ion channel","B":"G protein-coupled receptor","C":"Nuclear hormone receptor","D":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","E":"Serine threonine kinase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase required for proliferation, survival, and directed migration of enteric neural crest precursors. Ligand-gated channels, G protein-coupled receptors, nuclear hormone receptors, and serine threonine kinases are other signaling classes not encoded by RET. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gain-of-function RET mutations cause MEN 2A and 2B, the opposite of the loss of function in megacolon (First Aid Endocrinology, MEN syndromes). COMLEX: RET is discussed further with neural crest development in Langman Ch. 18.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Which bowel segment is involved in nearly every case of aganglionic megacolon?","choices":{"A":"Rectum","B":"Cecum","C":"Appendix","D":"Jejunum","E":"Ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally for a variable distance, so the rectum is involved in nearly all cases. The cecum and appendix are affected only in long-segment or total colonic disease, and the jejunum and ileum only in rare extended forms. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: suction rectal biopsy is the diagnostic test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: in about 80% the defect extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid, and in about 3% the whole colon is affected (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen. His pulse is 112/min and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lactate is 4.8 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the first anterior branch of the aorta below the celiac trunk, just past its origin. Which colonic segment is at greatest risk of infarction?","choices":{"A":"Descending colon","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Distal transverse colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The occluded vessel is the superior mesenteric artery, which supplies midgut colon: cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, and distal third of the transverse colon are hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute mesenteric ischemia classically follows embolism from atrial fibrillation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: pain out of proportion to examination is the early bedside clue (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman undergoes open aortic aneurysm repair during which the vessel arising from the anterior aorta at the level of L3 is ligated. Two days later she has left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea. Her pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 128/74 mm Hg. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from just beyond the left colic flexure to the rectosigmoid. Which other structure normally depends on the same arterial territory?","choices":{"A":"Lower third of the anal canal","B":"Cecum and vermiform appendix","C":"Proximal two-thirds of transverse colon","D":"Upper two-thirds of the anal canal","E":"Terminal ileum and ileocecal valve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ligated vessel is the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, which supplies the distal transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper two-thirds of the anal canal. The lower third of the anal canal is ectodermal and supplied by inferior rectal branches, while the cecum, appendix, proximal transverse colon, and terminal ileum are midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia after aortic surgery reflects loss of inferior mesenteric inflow (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal arteries are the named branches of this vessel (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man is admitted with septic shock, and his blood pressure falls to 78/42 mm Hg for several hours. Two days later he has crampy left upper quadrant pain and passes maroon stool. His pulse is 104/min. Serum lactate is 3.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Colonoscopy shows segmental ischemia centered near the left colic flexure. The ischemic region lies at the watershed between the terminal branches of which arteries?","choices":{"A":"Ileocolic and right colic arteries","B":"Middle colic and left colic arteries","C":"Right colic and middle colic arteries","D":"Left colic and sigmoidal arteries","E":"Sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The left colic flexure region lies near the midgut–hindgut boundary, where terminal middle colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery meet left colic branches of the inferior mesenteric artery. The ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic pairs lie within midgut territory, and the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal pairs lie within hindgut territory. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are the classic watershed sites for colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: hypotension rather than occlusion is the usual cause of this pattern (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has 12 hours of colicky abdominal pain, distension, and vomiting. She has had two similar milder episodes. Her pulse is 108/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. CT shows a dilated cecum displaced into the left upper quadrant with a whirl of mesenteric vessels at its base. Which anatomic feature of her colon most likely allowed this twist?","choices":{"A":"Elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon","B":"Narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve","C":"Persistent free mesentery of the ascending colon","D":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum","E":"Thickened teniae coli of the cecum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Failure of the ascending colon to fuse with the posterior wall leaves a free mesentery, so the mobile cecum and colon can twist around their vascular pedicle and obstruct. An elongated sigmoid mesentery predisposes to sigmoid twisting rather than a displaced cecum, a narrow ileocecal valve does not cause rotation, rectal aganglionosis causes functional obstruction, and teniae do not create mobility. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: volvulus of the cecum and colon can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: volvulus is one of the four major causes of intestinal obstruction, most often in the sigmoid colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — intestinal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with no prior abdominal surgery has intermittent cramping pain and bilious vomiting for 2 days. His pulse is 96/min and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. CT shows a cluster of dilated small bowel loops lying behind the right colon within an abnormal peritoneal recess. Which developmental error most likely produced this recess?","choices":{"A":"Incomplete fusion of the ascending mesocolon","B":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus","C":"Failed return of the herniated midgut","D":"Reversed rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incomplete or irregular fusion of the ascending mesocolon with the posterior wall leaves retrocolic pockets in which small intestine can become trapped and obstruct. A vitelline duct remnant causes a diverticulum or fistula, failed return of the midgut produces an umbilical defect, reversed rotation places the duodenum anterior to the transverse colon, and the sigmoid does not descend in the same way. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hernias and adhesions are leading causes of small bowel obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: internal hernias can occur without surgical history when mesenteric fusion is abnormal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has 18 hours of fever, anorexia, and right upper quadrant pain. His temperature is 38.4°C (101.1°F) and pulse is 110/min. Leukocyte count is 15,800/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3). Ultrasonography shows a normal gallbladder and an inflamed appendix lying beneath the right lobe of the liver at the end of a high-riding colon. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":{"A":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","B":"Caudal descent of the cecal bud","C":"Fusion of the descending mesocolon","D":"Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen","E":"Return of loops from the umbilical cord"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After rotation the cecum lies in the right upper quadrant and normally descends into the right iliac fossa; when it fails to descend, the cecum and appendix stay subhepatic and appendicitis presents with right upper quadrant pain. Failed rotation places the colon on the left, descending mesocolic fusion affects the left side, the appendix does not undergo recanalization, and failed return causes an umbilical defect. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: periumbilical pain migrating to McBurney point is the classic presentation when the appendix is in its usual position (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: atypical appendix positions delay diagnosis and raise perforation risk (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — appendicitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has a flat perineum with no anal opening. His vital signs are normal. Over the next day, greenish particulate material appears in his urine. Imaging confirms a rectourinary fistula. Which embryologic error most likely caused this malformation?","choices":{"A":"Arrested caudal growth of the urorectal septum","B":"Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm","C":"Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen","D":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum","E":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the cloaca is too small or the urorectal septum does not extend far enough caudally, the hindgut opening shifts anteriorly into the urethra, producing a rectourethral fistula. Proctodeal ectoderm forms the anal pit, the rectum is not described as recanalizing, neural crest loss causes megacolon without a fistula, and a vitelline duct remnant opens at the umbilicus. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: meconium in the urine of a newborn boy signals a rectourinary tract communication (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: anorectal malformations often occur with vertebral, cardiac, renal, and limb defects in VACTERL association (First Aid Genetics, VACTERL).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has no anal opening in its normal position. On examination, meconium is seen passing from the vaginal introitus. Her vital signs are normal and the abdomen is mildly distended. Abnormal partitioning between which two embryonic compartments most likely caused this finding?","choices":{"A":"Allantoic duct and yolk sac","B":"Midgut and hindgut loops","C":"Urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","D":"Mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts","E":"Bladder and the urachus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The urorectal septum normally divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus anteriorly and the primitive anorectal canal posteriorly; incomplete separation leaves a rectovaginal fistula. The allantois and yolk sac are extraembryonic, the midgut–hindgut junction is in the transverse colon, the genital ducts form internal reproductive organs, and a urachal defect drains urine at the umbilicus. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectovaginal fistula is the female counterpart of the rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cloaca is endoderm-lined and bounded ventrally by surface ectoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has not passed meconium, and his abdomen is distended. A normally located anal dimple is present, and a thin bluish bulging membrane is seen at its center. No meconium appears in the urine or on the perineum, and imaging shows no fistula. The persistent tissue separates the hindgut from which structure?","choices":{"A":"Allantois","B":"Urachus","C":"Phallus","D":"Bladder","E":"Anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Imperforate anus occurs when the anal membrane fails to break down, so the hindgut stays closed off from the anal pit, the ectodermal depression of the proctodeum. The allantois and its remnant, the urachus, run from the bladder toward the umbilicus, the phallus forms from the genital tubercle, and the bladder forms from the urogenital sinus anterior to the septum. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated membrane without a fistula distinguishes this lesion from septal defects (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: the anal membrane is the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — intestinal atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a painful, bleeding perianal lesion for 3 months. He has a history of HIV infection. His vital signs are normal. Anoscopy shows an ulcerated mass entirely below the pectinate line, and biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. Which artery is the main arterial supply to the region of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal artery","B":"Middle colic artery","C":"Sigmoidal artery","D":"Inferior rectal artery","E":"Median sacral artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the anal canal is ectodermal and supplied by the inferior rectal arteries from the internal pudendal arteries. The superior rectal artery supplies the endodermal canal above the line, and the middle colic, sigmoidal, and median sacral arteries supply other territories. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: HIV and HPV infection raise the risk of anal squamous cancer (general knowledge; no First Aid anal-cancer entry verified).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has painless bright red blood coating her stool for 2 weeks. Her vital signs are normal. Hemoglobin is 13.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions arising above the pectinate line. Biopsy of the mucosa overlying these cushions would most likely show which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","B":"Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells","C":"Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium with ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Above the pectinate line the mucosa is endodermal and lined by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells and intestinal glands. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids are painless because the region above the line has visceral innervation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hemorrhoids). COMLEX: tumors above the line are typically adenocarcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — hemorrhoids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has sharp anal pain with each bowel movement and streaks of blood on the toilet paper for 3 weeks. His vital signs are normal. Examination shows a posterior midline tear of the anoderm extending from the outer margin to just below the pectinate line. The epithelium at the site of this tear derives from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Endoderm of the hindgut","B":"Mesoderm of the urorectal septum","C":"Endoderm of the allantois","D":"Ectoderm of the neural tube","E":"Ectoderm of the anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lower third of the anal canal, below the pectinate line, derives from surface ectoderm of the proctodeum, the anal pit, which is why it is squamous and somatically sensitive. Hindgut endoderm forms the canal above the line, the septal mesoderm forms the perineal body, allantoic endoderm relates to the bladder, and neural tube ectoderm forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anal fissures are painful because they lie below the line in somatically innervated tissue (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anal fissure). COMLEX: most fissures occur in the posterior midline (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium since birth and has progressive abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. His temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F) and pulse is 150/min. Rectal examination produces an explosive release of stool. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectosigmoid with a markedly dilated colon proximal to it. Which mechanism best explains the dilation of the proximal colon?","choices":{"A":"Tonic contraction of the distal aganglionic segment","B":"Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment","C":"Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium","D":"Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment","E":"Fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lost enteric ganglion cells normally signal relaxation, so the distal aganglionic segment stays hypercontracted, peristalsis fails to pass, and content backs up into the normally innervated proximal colon, which dilates. The dilated segment has ganglia, inspissated meconium causes meconium ileus, excess ganglia do not cause this picture, and ischemic strictures are acquired. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the narrow segment is the diseased one, and the dilated bowel above it is normal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — defecation reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-week-old girl has chronic constipation and abdominal distension, and her older brother required colonic surgery in infancy. Her vital signs are normal. Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function variant in RET. Which developmental process was most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the colonic lumen","B":"Partitioning of the cloaca","C":"Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle","D":"Rotation of the primary intestinal loop","E":"Craniocaudal migration of enteric precursors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loss of RET signaling arrests the proximal-to-distal migration of neural crest precursors along the bowel or causes their premature death, leaving a distal aganglionic segment. Recanalization defects cause atresia or stenosis, cloacal partitioning defects cause fistulas, smooth muscle differentiation is a mesodermal process, and rotation defects cause malrotation. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most distal bowel is affected because it is reached last by migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: in about 80% of cases the aganglionic segment extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid (Langman Ch. 15); EDNRB–EDN3 also supports enteric crest migration (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-day-old boy has delayed passage of meconium and abdominal distension. His vital signs are normal. A suction biopsy is taken from the rectum 2 cm above the pectinate line. Absence of ganglion cells in which layers of the bowel wall would confirm the suspected diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal crypts and lamina propria","B":"Muscularis mucosae and serosa","C":"Serosa and mesenteric fat","D":"Submucosal and myenteric plexuses","E":"Celiac and mesenteric ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neural crest cells form the enteric ganglia of the submucosal and myenteric plexuses, and their absence in a rectal biopsy confirms aganglionic megacolon. The mucosa, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, serosa, and mesenteric fat do not house enteric ganglia, and the celiac and mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral sympathetic ganglia outside the wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosed by absence of ganglion cells on rectal suction biopsy; RET mutation in the REcTum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: biopsies are taken ≥2 cm above the pectinate line to avoid the normal hypoganglionic zone (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — GI wall layers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has ischemia limited to the sigmoid colon. Which arteries most directly supply this segment?","choices":{"A":"Middle colic arteries","B":"Right colic arteries","C":"Ileocolic arteries","D":"Sigmoidal arteries","E":"Inferior rectal arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sigmoid colon is hindgut and is supplied by the sigmoidal branches of the inferior mesenteric artery. The middle colic, right colic, and ileocolic arteries supply midgut colon, and the inferior rectal arteries supply the anal canal below the pectinate line. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rectosigmoid junction is a watershed site in colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic artery supplies the descending colon (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — colonic ischemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman is found to have an abnormally mobile cecum and ascending colon on imaging. Which acute complication is she predisposed to?","choices":{"A":"Cecal volvulus","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Intussusception","D":"Diverticulitis","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An ascending colon that keeps a free mesentery is mobile and can twist around its vascular pedicle, causing volvulus of the cecum and colon. Sigmoid volvulus relates to a long sigmoid mesentery, intussusception involves telescoping bowel, diverticulitis follows acquired outpouchings, and angiodysplasia is a vascular malformation. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: untreated volvulus can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: cecal twisting appears on imaging as a dilated cecum displaced from the right lower quadrant (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — volvulus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has no anal opening and meconium in his urine. Into which structure does the abnormal tract most likely open?","choices":{"A":"Umbilicus","B":"Ureter","C":"Seminal vesicle","D":"Sacral skin","E":"Urethra"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A urorectal septum that stops short shifts the hindgut opening anteriorly, and in boys it commonly opens into the urethra as a rectourethral fistula. The umbilicus relates to vitelline or urachal remnants, and the ureter, seminal vesicle, and sacral skin are not typical sites. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the female counterpart opens into the vagina (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has an adenocarcinoma of the anal canal just above the pectinate line. Which artery mainly supplies this site?","choices":{"A":"Inferior rectal artery","B":"Superior rectal artery","C":"Middle colic artery","D":"Ileocolic artery","E":"Obturator artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The canal above the pectinate line is endodermal hindgut and is supplied by the superior rectal artery, the continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery. The inferior rectal artery supplies the canal below the line, the middle colic and ileocolic arteries supply midgut colon, and the obturator artery supplies the thigh. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors above the line drain to internal iliac nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: venous drainage above the line reaches the portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn with aganglionic megacolon has normal RET. Which other receptor pathway supports enteric neural crest migration?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and Frizzled","B":"Sonic hedgehog and Patched","C":"Endothelin 3 and EDNRB","D":"Notch and Delta","E":"BMP and SMAD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Besides RET, the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway supports proliferation and migration of enteric neural crest precursors, and defects in it also cause aganglionosis. Wnt, hedgehog, Notch, and BMP pathways pattern other parts of the gut and embryo. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric ganglia lie in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a polyp in the descending colon. Its epithelium arose from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Midgut endoderm","B":"Splanchnic mesoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Neural crest","E":"Hindgut endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative, and its epithelium comes from hindgut endoderm. Midgut endoderm lines the cecum through proximal transverse colon, splanchnic mesoderm forms muscle and connective tissue, surface ectoderm forms the lower anal canal, and neural crest forms enteric ganglia. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut epithelium and glandular parenchyma are endodermal throughout the tract (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: hindgut endoderm also lines the bladder and urethra (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — origins of gut components","isHighYield":true}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"In which part of the small intestine are goblet cells most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum","B":"Distal duodenum","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Middle jejunum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Goblet cells increase in number from the duodenum to the terminal ileum. The duodenum and jejunum have fewer of them. The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak goblet cell density, which is reached toward the distal ileum. The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; their density rises steadily to its highest in the ileum. The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall plicae and villi, not for maximal goblet cell density, which is ileal. The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, where mucus-secreting cells reach their highest density. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: on routine H&E, the mucinogen-filled apex of a goblet cell looks empty because the mucinogen is lost during preparation (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak ","B":"The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; t","C":"The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall","E":"The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, wh"}},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"What portion of the small intestine does the jejunum make up?","choices":{"A":"Lower three-fifths of the small intestine","B":"Middle one-fifth of the small intestine","C":"Upper two-fifths of the small intestine","D":"Upper one-tenth of the small intestine","E":"Lower one-fifth of the small intestine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The jejunum is about 2.5 m long and makes up the upper two-fifths of the small intestine. The ileum is the lower three-fifths, and the duodenum is about 25 cm long. The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine; it is the upper two-fifths, about 2.5 m long. One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 cm), not the 2.5-m jejunum. The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is three-fifths, not a one-fifth jejunal segment. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the small intestine is the longest part of the digestive tract, at more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the ileum ends at the ileocecal junction, where the distal ileum joins the cecum (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine; it is t","D":"One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 c","E":"The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is"}},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are plicae circulares most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum next to the pylorus","B":"Middle and distal ileum segments","C":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve","D":"Cecum and proximal ascending colon","E":"Distal duodenum and proximal jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Plicae circulares are most numerous in the distal duodenum and the beginning of the jejunum. They become smaller and fewer by the middle of the ileum; the lecture notes likewise describe the jejunum as having the tallest, most numerous plicae and the distal ileum as markedly reduced. Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the pylorus, so the pylorus-adjacent duodenum lacks them. At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height and frequency. The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altogether; these folds belong to the small intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on oral contrast, the jejunum has a feathered look because its plicae circulares are taller and more numerous than the ileum's (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: these circular folds are permanent ridges that extend one-half to two-thirds of the way around the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the p","C":"At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height ","D":"The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altoget"}},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides the major amplification of the small intestinal luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares folds","B":"Mucosal intestinal villi","C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn","D":"Enterocyte microvilli","E":"Lateral cell plications"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Microvilli of the enterocytes provide the largest amplification of the luminal surface (20- to 600-fold), with several thousand per cell. Plicae and villi amplify it less, and lateral plications enlarge the lateral membrane. Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Paneth cells, not the main surface amplifier, which is the microvillus brush border. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a malabsorption workup, steatorrhea can be screened for with a Sudan stain for fecal fat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: under the light microscope, the closely packed microvilli form a striated border on the apex of the enterocyte (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Panet"}},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"By roughly how much do microvilli increase the apical surface area of an enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Up to about 600-fold","B":"Up to about 6-fold","C":"Up to about 60-fold","D":"Up to about 6,000-fold","E":"Up to about 60,000-fold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Microvilli increase the apical surface area of an enterocyte by as much as 600 times. The other values are one or more orders of magnitude off. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villi project 0.5–1.5 mm into the lumen and cover the whole small intestine, which gives it a velvety look (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: plicae circulares first appear about 5–6 cm beyond the pylorus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein anchors the actin core at the tip of each microvillus?","choices":{"A":"Myosin I","B":"Spectrin","C":"Villin","D":"Dystrophin","E":"Titin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The actin microfilaments of the microvillus core are anchored to villin at the tip. Myosin I attaches them to the plasma membrane along the sides. Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchoring actin at the microvillus tip. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tight junctions in the ileum are relatively impermeable, so solutes must be moved across by active transport (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: elongated mitochondria cluster in the apical enterocyte cytoplasm, between the terminal web and the nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchor"}},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibacterial products are found in Paneth cell secretory vesicles?","choices":{"A":"Lysozyme and α-defensins","B":"Mucinogen and trefoil factors","C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase","D":"Secretin and cholecystokinin","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cell vesicles contain lysozyme, α-defensins, other glycoproteins, an arginine-rich protein, and zinc. Mucinogen comes from goblet cells, and the hormones come from enteroendocrine cells. Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell products, not Paneth cell contents. Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G-cell products, not Paneth cell antimicrobial granules. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lysozyme digests the cell walls of certain groups of bacteria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Paneth cells may occasionally be found in small numbers in the normal colon, and their number may rise in some diseases (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell product","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G"}},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the basal portion of a goblet cell intensely basophilic?","choices":{"A":"Abundant smooth ER with lipid droplets","B":"Dense packing of acidic mucinogen granules","C":"Stacked apical mitochondria with actin","D":"Large glycogen stores with many lysosomes","E":"Heterochromatic nucleus with extensive rER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The narrow basal stem holds a heterochromatic nucleus, extensive rER, and free ribosomes, which make it intensely basophilic. The mucinogen granules sit at the apex. Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing enterocytes, not the basophilic goblet cell stem. Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, and are lost on routine H&E. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a large Golgi apparatus forms a wide cup around newly formed mucinogen granules above the goblet cell nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: microvilli are more obvious on immature goblet cells in the deep half of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing","B":"Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, "}},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What activates the apical receptors of open-type intestinal enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"Stretch of the muscularis","B":"Taste receptor stimulation","C":"Vagal acetylcholine release","D":"Circulating thyroid hormone","E":"Lamina propria histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Open enteroendocrine cells have taste receptors on their apical membrane, and activating them triggers a G-protein cascade and peptide release. Closed cells lie mainly in the lower gland. Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric nervous system, not the activator of apical receptors on open enteroendocrine cells. Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-type cells sense lumen contents through apical taste receptors. Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical luminal receptors of open-type cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP released in the small intestine stimulates insulin release from the pancreas (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: somatostatin and histamine from enteroendocrine cells act as paracrine hormones that do not circulate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric n","C":"Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-t","E":"Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical lumi"}},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the small intestinal stem cell niche located?","choices":{"A":"Tips of the intestinal villi","B":"Middle third of each villus surface","C":"Within the muscularis mucosae layer","D":"Lower half of the intestinal gland","E":"Within the duodenal submucosal glands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the base of the intestinal gland, and the niche is restricted to the lower half of the gland. Cells then migrate upward onto the villus and are shed at the tip. Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are shed, not where stem cells live. The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; proliferation is confined to the crypt. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epithelial stem cell niche. Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus and do not host the intestinal stem cell niche. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in celiac disease, histology shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: the niche is also called the zone of cell replication (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are s","B":"The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; prolife","C":"The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epit","E":"Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus "}},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the renewal time for absorptive and goblet cells in the human small intestine?","choices":{"A":"4–6 days","B":"4–6 hours","C":"4–6 weeks","D":"4–6 months","E":"1–2 years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autoradiographic studies show that absorptive and goblet cells in the human small intestine are renewed every 4–6 days. Paneth cells live much longer, about 4 weeks. Four to 6 hours is far too fast; renewal of absorptive and goblet cells takes 4 to 6 days. Four to 6 months is far too slow for this rapidly renewing epithelium, which turns over in days. One to 2 years would not fit an epithelium renewed every 4 to 6 days. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging epithelial cells undergo apoptosis at the villus tip and slough into the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web may help close the gaps left when aging cells exfoliate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Four to 6 hours is far too fast; renewal of absorptive and g","D":"Four to 6 months is far too slow for this rapidly renewing e","E":"One to 2 years would not fit an epithelium renewed every 4 t"}},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Increased expression of which transcription factor commits crypt progenitor cells to the secretory lineage?","choices":{"A":"Notch1","B":"Math1","C":"Cdx2","D":"Pdx1","E":"Ptf1a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells committed to the secretory lineage (goblet, enteroendocrine, and Paneth cells) have increased Math1 expression. When Math1 is inhibited, cells follow the default pathway and become enterocytes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteroendocrine cells appear to divide only once before differentiating (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: enteroendocrine cells migrate with absorptive and goblet cells, but more slowly (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are Peyer patches preferentially located in the ileal wall?","choices":{"A":"Along the mesenteric border itself","B":"Within the inner muscularis externa","C":"Opposite the mesenteric attachment","D":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium","E":"Within the myenteric nerve plexus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peyer patches are large and numerous in the ileum and lie preferentially on the side of the intestine opposite the mesenteric attachment. They sit in the lamina propria and often extend into the submucosa. Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesenteric border. Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not within the muscularis externa. Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not beneath the serosa. The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layers, not the site of Peyer patches. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: in gross specimens, these aggregated nodules look like clusters of white specks (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesente","B":"Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not w","D":"Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not b","E":"The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layer"}},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"From what is the secretory component of secretory IgA derived?","choices":{"A":"Plasma cell J chain peptide","B":"Cleaved polymeric Ig receptor","C":"Goblet cell mucin glycoprotein","D":"Paneth cell defensin precursor","E":"Hepatocyte bile acid carrier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the receptor-dIgA complex reaches the apical surface, the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor is cleaved. Its bound extracellular portion becomes the secretory component. The J chain comes from the plasma cell and links the IgA dimer. Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the secretory component of sIgA. Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unrelated to the secretory component. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the polymeric Ig receptor carries dIgA across the enterocyte by transcytosis; only at the apical surface is it cleaved, and the retained fragment is the secretory component (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the receptor is a 75-kDa transmembrane glycoprotein synthesized by enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the se","D":"Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unr"}},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody do most plasma cells in the intestinal lamina propria secrete?","choices":{"A":"Monomeric IgG","B":"Pentameric IgM","C":"Monomeric IgE","D":"Monomeric IgD","E":"Dimeric IgA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most lamina propria plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA rather than IgG. Some make pentameric IgM or IgE, and IgM can use a similar transcytotic route. IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most make dimeric IgA. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B cells stimulated in Peyer patch germinal centers become IgA-secreting plasma cells that settle in the lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: some IgE binds lamina propria mast cells and sensitizes them to luminal antigens (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most m"}},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with dyspepsia has an endoscopic biopsy taken just distal to the pylorus. It shows branched tubular submucosal glands whose secretion is highly alkaline. Besides protecting the mucosa, what does this secretion do?","choices":{"A":"Secretes lysozyme and defensins into the crypt lumens","B":"Brings chyme near the pancreatic enzyme pH optimum","C":"Absorbs conjugated bile acids from the passing chyme","D":"Produces intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 uptake","E":"Concentrates dimeric IgA for transport to the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Brunner gland secretion contains bicarbonate and neutral and alkaline glycoproteins. It neutralizes acid chyme and brings the intestinal contents close to the optimal pH for activating pancreatic enzymes. Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at the crypt base, not from Brunner glands. Brunner glands secrete; they do not absorb bile acids, which is an ileal function. Intrinsic factor is a gastric parietal cell product, not a Brunner gland secretion. Dimeric IgA is transported by enterocytes via pIgR, not concentrated by Brunner glands. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Submucosa, Fig. 17.25; Serosa). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bicarbonate from Brunner glands neutralizes acid, and pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion is stimulated by secretin (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI secretory products). COMLEX: the duct of each Brunner gland opens into the lumen of an intestinal crypt (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at the crypt b","C":"Brunner glands secrete; they do not absorb bile acids, which","D":"Intrinsic factor is a gastric parietal cell product, not a B","E":"Dimeric IgA is transported by enterocytes via pIgR, not conc"}},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a small bowel resection for trauma. The specimen shows tall villi and tall, closely spaced circular folds. It has no submucosal glands and no large lymphoid aggregates. Which segment was removed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Jejunum","D":"Appendix","E":"Rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prominent, numerous plicae and tall villi without Brunner glands or Peyer patches identify the jejunum. Submucosal glands mark the duodenum, Peyer patches mark the ileum, and the colon lacks villi. The appendix is large bowel with no villi or tall plicae, so it does not fit this specimen. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileum contains Peyer patches, and its plicae circulares are limited to its proximal part (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the jejunum gradually changes its morphology as it becomes the ileum, with no sharp boundary (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The appendix is large bowel with no villi or tall plicae, so"}},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has a stab wound that injures the retroperitoneal part of his duodenum. Which layer forms the outer covering of this segment?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Submucosa","D":"Muscularis","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parts of the duodenum in the retroperitoneum contact the posterior abdominal wall and have an adventitia. The intraperitoneal small intestine is covered by serosa, a mesothelium with underlying connective tissue. Submucosa is an internal layer beneath the mucosa, not the outer covering. The muscularis externa lies inside the outer covering rather than forming it. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Submucosa, Fig. 17.25; Serosa). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum is about 25 cm long and is the first, shortest, and widest part of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the myenteric plexus between the two muscularis externa layers contains postsynaptic parasympathetic neuron cell bodies (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"C":"Submucosa is an internal layer beneath the mucosa, not the o","D":"The muscularis externa lies inside the outer covering rather"}},{"id":"sdl23_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea and iron-deficiency anemia. Duodenal biopsy shows a flat mucosa with crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which surface-amplifying structure is lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae","B":"Villi","C":"Crypts","D":"Teniae","E":"Rugae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Villi are finger- or leaf-like mucosal projections with a lamina propria core, and celiac disease flattens them. Plicae have a submucosal core and persist. Microvilli are cell-surface structures. Crypts are hyperplastic, not lost, in this flat mucosa. Teniae coli are bands of colonic longitudinal muscle, not a small-intestinal surface amplifier. Rugae are gastric folds, not a duodenal surface structure. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); First Aid Gastrointestinal (celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and/or proximal jejunum and is linked to HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac serology includes IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Crypts are hyperplastic, not lost, in this flat mucosa.","D":"Teniae coli are bands of colonic longitudinal muscle, not a ","E":"Rugae are gastric folds, not a duodenal surface structure."}},{"id":"sdl23_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has edema and fatty stools. Biopsy shows dilated central lymphatic capillaries in his villi. Why do absorbed lipoprotein particles normally enter these vessels and not the villus blood capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries lack endothelial fenestrations","B":"Lymphatic capillaries actively pump in triglyceride","C":"Portal venous blood rapidly degrades fat particles","D":"Lipoproteins cross the tight junctions into lymph","E":"Particles are too large for the blood capillaries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sugars, amino acids, and other solutes diffuse into the fenestrated capillaries under the epithelium. Lipoprotein particles are too large to enter blood vessels, so they enter the lacteal. Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, yet chylomicrons are still too large to pass those fenestrations. Lacteal flow is driven by intermittent contraction of muscularis mucosae fibers, not active triglyceride pumping into lymphatics. Chylomicrons leave enterocytes by basolateral exocytosis and cross between endothelial cells into the lacteal, not through epithelial tight junctions. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an enterocyte packages resynthesized lipid as chylomicrons and discharges it into the intercellular space (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: smooth muscle from the muscularis mucosae runs alongside each lacteal and may squeeze lymph out of it (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, yet chylomicrons a","B":"Lacteal flow is driven by intermittent contraction of muscul","D":"Chylomicrons leave enterocytes by basolateral exocytosis and"}},{"id":"sdl23_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man in a nutrition study has a jejunal biopsy during active absorption. Electron microscopy shows widened spaces between the lateral surfaces of adjacent enterocytes. What structural change explains this?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of the apical tight junction protein strands","B":"Loss of the underlying enterocyte basal lamina sheet","C":"Separation of interdigitating cell-surface folds","D":"Shedding of aged enterocytes at the villus tips","E":"Fusion of mucinogen granules at the cell apex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterocytes have flattened lateral processes (plicae) that interdigitate with those of neighboring cells. During active absorption these separate, enlarging the intercellular compartment and driving flow into the lamina propria. Shedding at villus tips is normal turnover, not a cause of widened lateral intercellular spaces. Mucinogen exocytosis is a goblet cell apical event, unrelated to enterocyte lateral spaces. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the duodenum and jejunum, hypotonic contents allow water and small solutes to cross leaky tight junctions by solvent drag (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web spreads the microvilli apart and exposes more surface area (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Shedding at villus tips is normal turnover, not a cause of w","E":"Mucinogen exocytosis is a goblet cell apical event, unrelate"}},{"id":"sdl23_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with chronic diarrhea has a jejunal biopsy. At the base of the intestinal glands, H&E shows cells with basophilic basal cytoplasm and large, intensely eosinophilic, refractile apical vesicles. What probably causes the eosinophilia?","choices":{"A":"Heme-rich cytochromes","B":"Glycogen granule stores","C":"Neutral mucin polymer","D":"Arginine-rich protein","E":"Iron-ferritin deposits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These are Paneth cells. An arginine-rich protein in their secretory vesicles is probably responsible for the intense acidophilia. Mucin is lost on routine sections, and glycogen and ferritin do not explain the staining. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: zinc is also stored in these refractile apical vesicles (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has an ileal biopsy for chronic diarrhea. Routine H&E shows many epithelial cells with pale, empty-looking apical cups and narrow basal stems. Why does the apex of these cells look empty?","choices":{"A":"Lipid droplets dissolve in alcohol","B":"Mucinogen is lost during preparation","C":"Glycogen leaches out during fixation","D":"Apical vacuoles are filled with water","E":"Dying cells lose their organelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Goblet cell mucinogen is water soluble and is lost during routine H&E preparation, so the mucinogen-filled apex looks empty. TEM shows a large mass of mucinogen granules there. Goblet cell apices hold mucinogen, not lipid droplets. The apical cup holds mucinogen granules, not glycogen. The apex is packed with membrane-bound mucinogen granules, not water-filled vacuoles. These are viable mucin-secreting cells with rER-rich basal stems, not dying cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus, the squamous epithelium is replaced by nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the apical mucinogen mass in a goblet cell pushes on and distorts the shape of neighboring cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Goblet cell apices hold mucinogen, not lipid droplets.","C":"The apical cup holds mucinogen granules, not glycogen.","D":"The apex is packed with membrane-bound mucinogen granules, n","E":"These are viable mucin-secreting cells with rER-rich basal s"}},{"id":"sdl23_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has a well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor in her duodenum. From which epithelial cell type does this tumor most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Enteroendocrine cells","B":"Absorptive enterocytes","C":"Intermediate goblet cells","D":"Microfold epithelial cells","E":"Myoepithelial basket cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroendocrine cells make paracrine and endocrine peptides, including CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin, and are the epithelial source of neuroendocrine tumors. Enterocytes absorb, and M cells transport antigen. Goblet cells secrete mucin and are not the source of hormone-producing neuroendocrine tumors. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin from the small intestine initiates gastric and intestinal motility (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: VIP, bombesin, and enkephalins are neurocrine peptides secreted by submucosal and myenteric nerve cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Goblet cells secrete mucin and are not the source of hormone"}},{"id":"sdl23_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has watery diarrhea 5 days after starting cytotoxic chemotherapy. Biopsy shows short, denuded villi. Why is the small intestinal epithelium damaged so quickly?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells migrating upward onto the villi","B":"Slow turnover of the mature villus enterocyte layer","C":"Continuous replacement from dividing crypt cells","D":"Stem cells concentrated near the outer villus tips","E":"Mature enterocytes dividing on the villus tips"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Small intestinal absorptive and goblet cells are replaced every 4–6 days from a highly proliferative niche in the lower crypt. When chemotherapy blocks division, losses at the villus tip are not replaced. Paneth cells migrate downward to the crypt base, not upward onto villi. Villus enterocyte turnover is fast, about 4 to 6 days, not slow. Stem cells are in the lower crypt, not near villus tips. Mature villus enterocytes do not divide; they are shed at the tip and replaced from the crypt. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells in the niche usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to absorptive or goblet fates (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: cells shed at the villus tip are replaced by cells migrating upward from the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth cells migrate downward to the crypt base, not upward ","B":"Villus enterocyte turnover is fast, about 4 to 6 days, not s","D":"Stem cells are in the lower crypt, not near villus tips.","E":"Mature villus enterocytes do not divide; they are shed at th"}},{"id":"sdl23_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man in a research study has radiolabeled crypt cells. Days later, most labeled cells are on the villi. One labeled population has migrated downward to the gland base, lives about 4 weeks, and no longer divides. Which cells are these?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Enterocytes","D":"Stem cells","E":"Plasma cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cells come from the crypt stem cells, migrate downward to the bottom of the gland, live about 4 weeks, and no longer divide once recognizable. Absorptive and goblet cells migrate upward. Enterocytes migrate upward to the villus tip and turn over in 4 to 6 days. Stem cells keep dividing in the crypt niche, unlike the nondividing labeled cells here. Plasma cells are lamina propria immune cells, not epithelial cells derived from crypt stem cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dead Paneth cells are replaced by differentiation of a nearby committed cell in the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: stem cells in the crypt base give rise to every mature cell type of the intestinal epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Enterocytes migrate upward to the villus tip and turn over i","D":"Stem cells keep dividing in the crypt niche, unlike the nond","E":"Plasma cells are lamina propria immune cells, not epithelial"}},{"id":"sdl23_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has a genetic variant that blocks the transcription factor driving secretory fate in his intestinal stem cell niche. Which cells would differentiating crypt cells mainly become?","choices":{"A":"Mucus-secreting goblet cells","B":"Antimicrobial Paneth cells","C":"Hormone-secreting endocrine cells","D":"Absorptive enterocytes","E":"Antigen-sampling microfold cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Inhibiting the secretory transcription factor sends cells down the default pathway into absorptive enterocytes. Goblet, Paneth, and enteroendocrine cells form the secretory lineage. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells committed to the absorptive fate lose their secretory granules and gather mitochondria, rER, and ribosomes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: intermediate cells committed to the goblet fate develop a small rounded cluster of granules under the apical membrane (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man develops typhoid fever after travel. The bacteria crossed his ileal epithelium where it covers large lymphatic nodules. Which route did they most likely use?","choices":{"A":"Microfold cell transcytosis","B":"Paracellular tight junctions","C":"Goblet cell mucus exocytosis","D":"Paneth cell phagocytosis","E":"Lacteal lymphatic uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M cells over Peyer patches bind macromolecules and Gram-negative bacteria to GP2 receptors. They endocytose them and release them into a basolateral pocket holding immune cells. Typhoid bacteria crossing follicle-associated epithelium use M-cell transcytosis, not tight junctions between enterocytes. Goblet cell exocytosis releases mucus outward and is not an entry route for bacteria. Paneth cells secrete antimicrobial granules and do not phagocytose and transport bacteria. Lacteals take up chylomicrons from the villus core, not bacteria across the epithelium. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches contain microfold cells that sample antigens and present them to immune cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: M cells have microfolds and a thin glycocalyx on their apical surface, not microvilli (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Typhoid bacteria crossing follicle-associated epithelium use","C":"Goblet cell exocytosis releases mucus outward and is not an ","D":"Paneth cells secrete antimicrobial granules and do not phago","E":"Lacteals take up chylomicrons from the villus core, not bact"}},{"id":"sdl23_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has recurrent giardiasis. Serum IgA is undetectable (reference range 70–400 mg/dL), and IgG and IgM are normal. Which mucosal defense is mainly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Lysis of luminal bacteria by Paneth cell enzymes","B":"Sensitization of the lamina propria mast cells","C":"Opsonization of invading bacteria for neutrophils","D":"Blocking of microbial adhesion to the epithelium","E":"Activation of the classical complement pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Luminal secretory IgA binds antigens, toxins, and microbes. It blocks their attachment and invasion by inhibiting motility, causing aggregation, or masking adhesion sites. Mast cell sensitization uses IgE, and opsonization is mainly an IgG role. Paneth cell lysozyme is innate and independent of IgA, so it is not what is lost. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretory IgA binds an HIV envelope glycoprotein and prevents viral attachment and internalization (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the GALT works as an immunologic barrier along the whole length of the gastrointestinal tract (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth cell lysozyme is innate and independent of IgA, so it"}},{"id":"sdl23_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has recurrent enteric infections. Her lamina propria plasma cells secrete normal J-chain-linked IgA dimers, but her intestinal secretions lack IgA. Which step most likely fails?","choices":{"A":"Class switching of mucosal B cells to the IgA isotype","B":"Linking of IgA monomers together by the J chain","C":"Homing of IgA plasma cells to the lamina propria","D":"Antigen uptake by the Peyer patch dendritic cells","E":"Receptor-mediated transcytosis across enterocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dimeric IgA binds a receptor on the basal enterocyte membrane and is endocytosed. It is carried across the cell to the apical surface and released into the lumen. Normal dimers with absent luminal IgA point to failed epithelial transport. Class switching is intact because plasma cells already make IgA dimers. J-chain linking is intact, since the dimers are described as normal. Homing is intact because IgA plasma cells are present in the lamina propria. Dendritic cell antigen uptake precedes B-cell activation; IgA is already being made here. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM molecules use a similar receptor-mediated transcytosis pathway to reach the mucosal surface (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: dendritic cells and lymphocytes in the M-cell recess sample luminal antigen and can stimulate specific antibody production (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Class switching is intact because plasma cells already make ","B":"J-chain linking is intact, since the dimers are described as","C":"Homing is intact because IgA plasma cells are present in the","D":"Dendritic cell antigen uptake precedes B-cell activation; Ig"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man is having a pancreaticoduodenectomy. How long is the first, shortest, and widest part of his small intestine?","choices":{"A":"About 5 cm","B":"About 2.5 m","C":"About 3.5 m","D":"About 25 cm","E":"About 60 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The duodenum is about 25 cm long. The jejunum is about 2.5 m and the ileum about 3.5 m long. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum ends at the duodenojejunal junction, where the jejunum begins (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the whole small intestine measures more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a normal duodenal biopsy during an anemia workup. How far do normal small intestinal villi project into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"About 0.5–1.5 mm","B":"About 5–15 µm","C":"About 50–150 µm","D":"About 5–15 mm","E":"About 5–15 cm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villi extend 0.5–1.5 mm from the mucosal surface into the lumen and give it a velvety look. Microvilli are micrometer-scale, and plicae are larger folds. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.18). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in lactose intolerance the villi look normal unless lactase loss is secondary to injury at the villus tips (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the core of each villus is an extension of the lamina propria containing fenestrated capillaries (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has an ileal biopsy examined by electron microscopy. In goblet cells, what is the thin rim of apical cytoplasm bearing microvilli around the mucinogen granules called?","choices":{"A":"Calyx","B":"Cuticle","C":"Theca","D":"Capsule","E":"Mantle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In goblet cells, microvilli are restricted to a thin rim of cytoplasm called the theca. It surrounds the apical and lateral parts of the mucinogen mass. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet cells, Fig. 17.22). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondria in goblet cells are concentrated in the basal cytoplasm (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the shape of the goblet cell, with a narrow basal stem under an apical granule mass, gives it its name (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has a jejunal biopsy taken for research. In the transit-amplifying compartment of the crypt, how many divisions do intermediate cells usually undergo before committing to an absorptive or goblet fate?","choices":{"A":"About ten divisions","B":"No further divisions","C":"Thirty to forty divisions","D":"Hundreds of divisions","E":"One or two divisions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Intermediate cells form the amplifying compartment of the niche. They usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to the absorptive or goblet fate. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Intermediate cells; Epithelial cell renewal). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells have short, irregular microvilli with long core filaments and many desmosomal junctions (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: small mucin-like granules form a central column in the supranuclear cytoplasm of intermediate cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old man has a jejunal biopsy for evaluation of diarrhea. Roughly how much of the normal intestinal mucosa is made up of loosely organized lymphatic tissue?","choices":{"A":"About one-fourth","B":"About one-tenth","C":"About one-half","D":"About two-thirds","E":"About nine-tenths"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About one-fourth of the gut mucosa is a loosely organized layer of lymphatic nodules, lymphocytes, macrophages, plasma cells, and eosinophils in the lamina propria. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT is prominent in the lamina propria). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymphocytes in the gut are also found between epithelial cells as well as in the lamina propria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: activated antigen-presenting cells migrate to lamina propria nodules, where new plasma cells arise (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has upper endoscopy for anemia. How far beyond the pylorus do the plicae circulares begin to appear?","choices":{"A":"About 5–6 mm","B":"About 5–6 cm","C":"About 50–60 cm","D":"About 1–2 m","E":"About 3–4 m"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Plicae circulares begin to appear about 5–6 cm beyond the pylorus. They are most numerous in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.17). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: plicae circulares are also called the valves of Kerckring (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: each circular fold contains a core of submucosa, unlike a villus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true}],"24":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"How many teniae coli run along the cecum and colon?","choices":{"A":"Two opposing bands","B":"Four paired bands","C":"Five irregular bands","D":"Three equal bands","E":"Six spiral bands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"There are three narrowed, thickened, equally spaced teniae coli, formed from the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa. Two bands is too few; the outer longitudinal muscle condenses into three equally spaced teniae. There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands. The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregular ones. The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six spiral bands. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: between the teniae, the outer longitudinal layer of the colon is an extremely thin sheet (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: omental appendices are small fat-filled peritoneal projections on the outer colonic surface (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two bands is too few; the outer longitudinal muscle condense","B":"There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands.","C":"The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregula","E":"The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six"}},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"In which parts of the large intestine are teniae coli absent?","choices":{"A":"Cecum, ascending, and transverse colon","B":"Transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon, cecum, and appendix","D":"Descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum","E":"Rectum, anal canal, and appendix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teniae coli are seen mainly in the cecum and colon. In the rectum, anal canal, and vermiform appendix, the outer longitudinal muscle is a uniformly thick layer, as in the small intestine. The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly where teniae coli are seen. The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teniae coli. The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix of this group lacks them. The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectum of this group lacks them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix differs from the cecum by having a uniform layer of longitudinal muscle in its muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the anal canal averages 4 cm and runs from the upper pelvic diaphragm to the anus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly whe","B":"The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teni","C":"The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix","D":"The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectu"}},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"What are omental appendices of the colon?","choices":{"A":"Fatty projections of the serosa","B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules","C":"Submucosal outpouchings of the wall","D":"Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle","E":"Sacculations between muscle bands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Omental appendices are small fatty projections of the serosa on the outer colonic surface. Teniae are muscle bands, and haustra are sacculations between them. Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, whereas omental appendices hang from the outer serosal surface. Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty serosal tags called omental appendices. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra coli are the visible sacculations between the teniae on the external surface of the cecum and colon (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colon is subdivided into ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid parts by anatomic location (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, w","C":"Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty "}},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing most dietary amino acids","B":"Secreting brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Reabsorbing water and electrolytes","D":"Transporting dimeric IgA to the lumen","E":"Producing antimicrobial defensins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells is reabsorbing water and electrolytes. They use the same Na+/K+-ATPase-driven system as small intestinal enterocytes. Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine, not by colonocytes, whose role is water and electrolyte reabsorption. Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycocalyx, unlike small intestinal enterocytes. Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent from the human large intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are essentially identical in morphology to small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the principal functions of the large intestine are electrolyte and water reabsorption and elimination of undigested waste (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine","B":"Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycoc","E":"Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent "}},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the ratio of columnar absorptive cells to goblet cells in most of the colon?","choices":{"A":"About 4:1","B":"About 1:1","C":"About 1:3","D":"About 20:1","E":"About 100:1"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Columnar absorptive cells predominate about 4:1 over goblet cells in most of the colon. The ratio approaches 1:1 near the rectum, where goblet cells increase. A 1:1 ratio is found only near the rectum, not in most of the colon. Goblet cells never outnumber absorptive cells 3:1; at most the ratio reaches 1:1 near the rectum. Twenty to one far overstates absorptive cell predominance, which is about 4:1. One hundred to one would leave the colon nearly goblet-free, yet goblet cells are more numerous than in the small intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: as absorptive cells are traced into the colonic glands they become fewer, while goblet cells increase (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 4:1 predominance of absorptive cells is not readily apparent in histologic sections (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A 1:1 ratio is found only near the rectum, not in most of th","C":"Goblet cells never outnumber absorptive cells 3:1; at most t","D":"Twenty to one far overstates absorptive cell predominance, w","E":"One hundred to one would leave the colon nearly goblet-free,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the turnover time of the glycocalyx secreted by colonic absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"16–24 minutes","B":"2–3 hours","C":"4–6 days","D":"3–4 weeks","E":"16–24 hours"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells secrete glycocalyx rapidly, with a turnover time of 16–24 hours in humans. Unlike in the small intestine, it has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes. Sixteen to 24 minutes is too fast; glycocalyx turnover is 16 to 24 hours. Two to 3 hours is too short for the 16–24-hour glycocalyx turnover. About 6 days is the turnover of the cells themselves, not of their glycocalyx. Three to 4 weeks matches enteroendocrine cell lifespan, not glycocalyx turnover. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colonic glycocalyx has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Na+/K+-ATPase is abundant in the lateral plasma membranes of colonic absorptive cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sixteen to 24 minutes is too fast; glycocalyx turnover is 16","B":"Two to 3 hours is too short for the 16–24-hour glycocalyx tu","C":"About 6 days is the turnover of the cells themselves, not of","D":"Three to 4 weeks matches enteroendocrine cell lifespan, not "}},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What part of the colonic intestinal gland forms the stem cell niche?","choices":{"A":"Upper third near the surface","B":"Lower third of the gland","C":"Middle third of the gland","D":"Entire length of the gland","E":"Surface cuff between glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the bottom of the intestinal gland, and the lower third of the gland is the stem cell niche. New cells then migrate up to the luminal surface. The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate toward shedding, not the niche. The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplifying zone are in the lower third. Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread along its whole length. The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are shed, not where they are generated. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: all mucosal epithelial cells of the large intestine come from stem cells at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the intermediate cell types in the lower third of the colonic gland are identical to those in the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate towa","C":"The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplif","D":"Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread alon","E":"The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are sh"}},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the turnover time of colonic enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"About 6 hours","B":"About 24 hours","C":"About 2 days","D":"About 1 year","E":"Up to 4 weeks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive and goblet cells turn over in about 6 days, whereas enteroendocrine cells last up to 4 weeks. Six hours is far shorter than the up-to-4-week life of colonic enteroendocrine cells. Twenty-four hours is too short for enteroendocrine cells, which live up to 4 weeks. Two days is shorter even than the 6-day turnover of absorptive and goblet cells. One year greatly exceeds the up-to-4-week lifespan of colonic enteroendocrine cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic epithelial turnover times are similar to those of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, cells migrating downward to become parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells turn over in about 1 year (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Six hours is far shorter than the up-to-4-week life of colon","B":"Twenty-four hours is too short for enteroendocrine cells, wh","C":"Two days is shorter even than the 6-day turnover of absorpti","D":"One year greatly exceeds the up-to-4-week lifespan of coloni"}},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where on the colonic surface are senile epithelial cells shed?","choices":{"A":"Base of each intestinal gland","B":"Rim of each gland opening","C":"Crests of the semilunar folds","D":"Between two adjacent glands","E":"Tips of the colonic villi"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Senile colonic epithelial cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed at the midpoint between two adjacent intestinal glands. The colon has no villi. The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made rather than shed. Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim of each opening. Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haustra, not the defined shedding site. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories of gland openings (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic gland openings on the luminal surface are arranged in an orderly pattern (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made ","B":"Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim","C":"Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haus"}},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer of the colon lie?","choices":{"A":"Between muscularis mucosae and submucosa","B":"Between the teniae and the circular muscle","C":"Under the epithelial basal lamina","D":"Within the core of each lymphatic nodule","E":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The collagen table is a thick layer of collagen and proteoglycans between the epithelial basal lamina and that of the fenestrated absorptive venous capillaries. The collagen table lies directly under the surface epithelium, not deep at the muscularis mucosae. The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far from the subepithelial collagen table. Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer. The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not beneath the serosa. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the collagen table participates in regulating water and electrolyte transport from the epithelium to the vascular compartment (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the absorptive venous capillaries under the colonic epithelium are fenestrated (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The collagen table lies directly under the surface epitheliu","B":"The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far f","D":"Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepit","E":"The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not benea"}},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"How thick is the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer in the normal human colon?","choices":{"A":"Up to about 5 µm","B":"Up to about 0.5 µm","C":"Up to about 50 µm","D":"Up to about 0.5 mm","E":"Up to about 5 mm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The collagen table is about 5 µm thick in the normal colon and can be up to 3 times thicker in hyperplastic colonic polyps. Half a micrometer is ten times too thin for the ~5-µm collagen table. Fifty micrometers is ten times the normal ~5-µm thickness, beyond even hyperplastic polyps. Half a millimeter is a hundredfold too thick for the ~5-µm collagen table. Five millimeters is a gross-scale thickness, far beyond the ~5-µm collagen table. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are generally benign, not neoplastic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the colonic lamina propria shares the basic components of the rest of the gut but has extra specializations (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Half a micrometer is ten times too thin for the ~5-µm collag","C":"Fifty micrometers is ten times the normal ~5-µm thickness, b","D":"Half a millimeter is a hundredfold too thick for the ~5-µm c","E":"Five millimeters is a gross-scale thickness, far beyond the "}},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"From what may macrophages in the core of the colonic lamina propria arise?","choices":{"A":"Circulating neutrophils","B":"Pericryptal fibroblasts","C":"Exhausted goblet cells","D":"Epithelial niche cells","E":"Muscularis mucosae cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pericryptal fibroblasts divide beneath the gland base and migrate upward with the epithelium. Near the surface most take on macrophage features, and lamina propria macrophages may be their terminal stage. Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surface, not macrophage precursors. Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina propria macrophages come from pericryptal fibroblasts. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pericryptal fibroblast sheath exists in both the large and small intestines (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: pericryptal fibroblasts divide adjacent to the epithelial stem cells at the bottom of the gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surf","D":"Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina p"}},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do colonic lymphatics from the gland bases first drain?","choices":{"A":"Lacteals in the lamina propria core","B":"Vessels reaching the luminal surface","C":"Plexus beneath the serosal surface","D":"Network within the muscularis mucosae","E":"Sinusoids of the regional lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lymphatics are generally absent from the lamina propria core, but occasional small vessels at the gland bases drain into a network in the muscularis mucosae. Lymph then passes to submucosal and muscularis externa plexuses and on to regional nodes. The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymphatics are absent near the surface. Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confined to gland bases. Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae, not directly to a subserosal plexus. Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, submucosal, and muscularis externa plexuses. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: no lymphatic vessels extend toward the luminal surface of the large intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymph leaves the colonic wall only after passing through plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymph","B":"Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confin","C":"Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae,","E":"Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, subm"}},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody marker is used to identify lymphatic endothelium in colonic lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"CD117","B":"CD34","C":"D2-40","D":"CK20","E":"S-100"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The monoclonal antibody D2-40 reacts with a 40-kDa O-linked sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium and is used to map lamina propria lymphatics. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the absence of lamina propria lymphatics was first found with light and electron microscopy of biopsy samples (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: D2-40 recognizes an O-linked sialoglycoprotein of about 40 kDa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"About what fraction of adenomatous polyps of the large intestine lie in the rectum and sigmoid colon?","choices":{"A":"About a tenth","B":"Almost half","C":"About a quarter","D":"About three-quarters","E":"About nine-tenths"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Almost 50% of adenomatous polyps of the large intestine are in the rectum and sigmoid colon, so rectosigmoidoscopy can detect them. A tenth understates it; almost 50% of adenomatous polyps lie in the rectum and sigmoid colon. A quarter understates the rectosigmoid share, which is almost half of adenomatous polyps. Three-quarters overstates the rectosigmoid share, which is almost half. Nine-tenths overstates it; only about half of adenomatous polyps are rectosigmoid. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: an adenomatous polyp is an intraepithelial neoplasm on a tissue mass protruding into the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"A tenth understates it; almost 50% of adenomatous polyps lie","C":"A quarter understates the rectosigmoid share, which is almos","D":"Three-quarters overstates the rectosigmoid share, which is a","E":"Nine-tenths overstates it; only about half of adenomatous po"}},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a screening colonoscopy, and a transverse colon biopsy is normal. Compared with an ileal biopsy, which mucosal feature distinguishes it?","choices":{"A":"Glands lined by simple columnar cells","B":"Lamina propria rich in lymphocytes","C":"Muscularis mucosae deep to the glands","D":"Smooth surface without villi","E":"Goblet cells among absorptive cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The large intestinal mucosa has a smooth surface with neither villi nor plicae circulares, and its straight tubular glands span the full mucosa. The other features are shared with the ileum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts of Lieberkühn with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the smooth colonic mucosa shows semilunar folds formed by contractions of the muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with long-standing ulcerative colitis has a barium study showing a lead-pipe colon. Which normal arrangement produces the sacculations she has lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares anchored in the submucosa","B":"Teniae bundles crossing circular muscle","C":"Fat lobules pushing out the serosal surface","D":"Lymphoid nodules distorting the gland rows","E":"Uniform longitudinal muscle around the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Muscle bundles from the teniae penetrate the inner circular layer at irregular intervals. These discontinuities let colonic segments contract independently and form the haustra. Plicae circulares are small intestinal submucosal folds and are absent in the colon. Fat lobules form omental appendices on the serosa, not haustral sacculations. Lymphoid nodules distort gland spacing in the mucosa but do not create haustra. A uniform longitudinal layer, as in the rectum and appendix, is exactly what lacks haustra. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa); First Aid Gastrointestinal (inflammatory bowel diseases). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is continuous colonic inflammation that involves the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: ulcerative colitis causes friable mucosa with superficial or deep ulcerations and crypt abscesses (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares are small intestinal submucosal folds and ","C":"Fat lobules form omental appendices on the serosa, not haust","D":"Lymphoid nodules distort gland spacing in the mucosa but do ","E":"A uniform longitudinal layer, as in the rectum and appendix,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a flexible sigmoidoscopy. In the upper rectum, the endoscopist sees several shelf-like mucosal folds. Which structures are these?","choices":{"A":"Colonic semilunar folds","B":"Longitudinal anal columns","C":"Transverse rectal folds","D":"Plicae circulares folds","E":"Gastric rugae-type folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The upper rectum is distinguished from the rest of the large intestine by transverse rectal folds. Anal columns are longitudinal folds of the upper anal canal, and semilunar folds lie in the colon. Plicae circulares belong to the small intestine; the large intestine lacks them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Rectum and anal canal). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectinate line forms where hindgut endoderm meets ectoderm (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the depressions between the anal columns are called anal sinuses (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"D":"Plicae circulares belong to the small intestine; the large i"}},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy. At the luminal surface, many tall, thin goblet cells hold only a few mucinogen granules in the central apical cytoplasm. What explains their appearance?","choices":{"A":"Secretion outpacing mucin synthesis","B":"Mucin loss during tissue processing","C":"Immature cells still in the niche","D":"Apoptosis of aging goblet cells","E":"Conversion into absorptive cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Goblet cells secrete mucus continuously as they migrate. At the surface the secretion rate exceeds the synthesis rate, so exhausted goblet cells appear. Processing loss empties goblet cells throughout, whereas these surface cells are exhausted from secretion. Immature niche cells lie deep in the gland base, not at the luminal surface. Apoptotic shedding occurs between gland openings, but the depleted granules reflect exhaustion, not cell death. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: caveolated tuft cells described in the colonic epithelium may be a form of exhausted goblet cell (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic goblet cells may mature deep in the gland, even within the replicative zone (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Processing loss empties goblet cells throughout, whereas the","C":"Immature niche cells lie deep in the gland base, not at the ","D":"Apoptotic shedding occurs between gland openings, but the de"}},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has biopsies at several colonoscopy sites. One site has absorptive and goblet cells in a ratio close to 1:1. Where was it most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Proximal cecum","B":"Lower rectum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Transverse colon","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Absorptive cells outnumber goblet cells about 4:1 in most of the colon, but the ratio approaches 1:1 near the rectum, where goblet cells increase. The cecum has the usual ~4:1 absorptive-to-goblet ratio, not 1:1. The ascending colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1:1. The transverse colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1:1. The descending colon still has the typical 4:1 ratio; 1:1 is reached only near the rectum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Rectum and anal canal). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectal mucosa resembles distal colonic mucosa, with straight tubular glands rich in goblet cells (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the rectum is the dilated distal portion of the alimentary canal (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum has the usual ~4:1 absorptive-to-goblet ratio, not","C":"The ascending colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1:","D":"The transverse colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1","E":"The descending colon still has the typical 4:1 ratio; 1:1 is"}},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic constipation has a colonic biopsy showing abundant goblet cells in the intestinal glands. What is the main role of their continuous secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing gastric acid in chyme","B":"Digesting the residual disaccharides","C":"Killing bacteria with lysozyme","D":"Absorbing short-chain fatty acids","E":"Easing the passage of firm contents"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic goblet cells, more numerous than in the small intestine, secrete mucin continuously to lubricate the bowel. This eases passage of increasingly solid contents. Colonic cells do not express digestive enzymes, so goblet secretion is not digestive. Lysozyme is a Paneth cell product, and Paneth cells are normally absent from the colon. Goblet cells secrete mucin; absorption is the job of columnar absorptive cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with low-fiber diets and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticulosis). COMLEX: elimination of semisolid to solid waste is aided by the large amount of mucus in the colonic glands (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic cells do not express digestive enzymes, so goblet se","C":"Lysozyme is a Paneth cell product, and Paneth cells are norm","D":"Goblet cells secrete mucin; absorption is the job of columna"}},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man in a research study has newly generated colonic crypt cells labeled. About how long after they begin migrating are these cells shed at the luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"About 5 hours","B":"About 5 weeks","C":"About 5 days","D":"About 5 months","E":"About 1 year"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Newly generated colonic cells undergo a few more divisions as they migrate up to the surface, where they are shed about 5 days later. Five hours is far too fast; shedding occurs about 5 days after generation. Five weeks is longer even than enteroendocrine lifespan; absorptive cells shed in about 5 days. Five months greatly exceeds the ~6-day colonic epithelial turnover. One year is irrelevant to an epithelium that turns over in about 6 days. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: as new colonic cells begin their upward migration, they continue dividing (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, surface and pit mucous cells turn over in 3–5 days (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Five hours is far too fast; shedding occurs about 5 days aft","B":"Five weeks is longer even than enteroendocrine lifespan; abs","D":"Five months greatly exceeds the ~6-day colonic epithelial tu","E":"One year is irrelevant to an epithelium that turns over in a"}},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy examined by electron microscopy. Deep in a gland, two cells with apical mucinogen granules are in mitosis. What does this show?","choices":{"A":"Mature goblet cells can still divide","B":"Mucinogen granules form during mitosis","C":"Exhausted surface cells reenter the cycle","D":"Paneth cells give rise to new goblets","E":"Absorptive cells convert into goblets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some intestinal cells keep dividing after they differentiate. Colonic goblet cells can mature deep in the gland, even in the replicative zone, and still divide. Exhausted cells are at the luminal surface awaiting shedding, not deep in the gland. Paneth cells are normally absent from the human large intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dividing intestinal cells typically move away from the basal lamina toward the gland lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: chromosomes of dividing goblet cells lack a surrounding nuclear membrane on EM (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"C":"Exhausted cells are at the luminal surface awaiting shedding","D":"Paneth cells are normally absent from the human large intest"}},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has hundreds of colonic adenomatous polyps, and his father had colectomy at age 35. Which initial epithelial event leads to small polyp formation in his colon?","choices":{"A":"Mutation of the KRAS proto-oncogene","B":"Deletion of the TP53 suppressor gene","C":"Methylation of the MLH1 repair gene","D":"Loss of the APC suppressor gene","E":"Gain of the RET tyrosine kinase gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the chromosomal instability pathway, colonic epithelial cells that lose the APC tumor suppressor gene develop small polyps. KRAS mutation then makes a benign adenoma, and p53 and DCC loss lead to carcinoma. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Folder 17.7, colorectal cancer); First Aid Gastrointestinal (familial adenomatous polyposis). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: familial adenomatous polyposis is an autosomal dominant APC mutation on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, familial adenomatous polyposis). COMLEX: about 98% of colorectal cancers are adenocarcinomas arising from glandular epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl24_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has a small hyperplastic colonic polyp removed. Under the surface epithelium, a layer of fibrillar protein and proteoglycans is about 15 µm thick. Which structure is thickened?","choices":{"A":"Basal lamina","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Pericryptal sheath","D":"Submucosa","E":"Collagen table"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The collagen table lies between the epithelial and capillary basal laminae and can be up to 3 times its normal thickness in hyperplastic colonic polyps. The basal lamina is a thin layer bounding the epithelium; the collagen table lies between basal laminae. The muscularis mucosae is smooth muscle at the mucosal base, not a subepithelial collagen layer. The pericryptal sheath is replicating fibroblasts around gland bases, not a fibrillar layer under surface epithelium. The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae, not directly under the surface epithelium. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mucosal polyps are usually small, look like normal mucosa, and are clinically insignificant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: submucosal polyps may include lipomas, leiomyomas, and fibromas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The basal lamina is a thin layer bounding the epithelium; th","B":"The muscularis mucosae is smooth muscle at the mucosal base,","C":"The pericryptal sheath is replicating fibroblasts around gla","D":"The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae, not directl"}},{"id":"sdl24_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy. Large lymphatic nodules distort the regular spacing of the glands and extend into the submucosa. What best explains this extensive lymphoid development?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of dietary antigens","B":"Thin goblet-cell mucus layer","C":"Absence of epithelial renewal","D":"Abundant luminal microbes","E":"Drainage of lacteal lymph flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colonic GALT is continuous with that of the terminal ileum and is more extensively developed. This probably reflects the large number and variety of microorganisms and noxious metabolic products in the colonic lumen. Colonic goblet cells are more numerous than in the small intestine, so a thin mucus layer is not the explanation. The colonic epithelium renews continuously about every 6 days. The colon has no villi and so no lacteal lymph flow. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease microscopically shows noncaseating granulomas and lymphoid aggregates (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: large colonic lymphatic nodules extend from the lamina propria into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic goblet cells are more numerous than in the small int","C":"The colonic epithelium renews continuously about every 6 day","E":"The colon has no villi and so no lacteal lymph flow."}},{"id":"sdl24_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has acute appendicitis without a fecalith. The resected appendix shows markedly enlarged lymphatic nodules with germinal centers. What is the most likely cause of his obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous scar tissue","B":"Lymphoid hyperplasia","C":"Carcinoid tumor mass","D":"Inspissated mucus plug","E":"Spasm of the teniae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most conspicuous feature of the appendix is its many lymphatic nodules extending into the submucosa. In children, appendicitis can be caused by lymphoid hyperplasia, whereas fecaliths are typical in adults. The appendix lacks teniae coli, so teniae spasm cannot obstruct it. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); First Aid Gastrointestinal (appendicitis). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendiceal obstruction causes early diffuse periumbilical pain through T8-T10 visceral afferents (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: the appendix is a common site of carcinoid, a tumor of enteroendocrine cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"E":"The appendix lacks teniae coli, so teniae spasm cannot obstr"}},{"id":"sdl24_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a pedunculated sigmoid polyp removed. It contains carcinoma confined to the mucosa above the muscularis mucosae. Why is endoscopic removal considered adequate?","choices":{"A":"Polyp stalks contain no blood vessels","B":"Basal lamina halts carcinoma cell spread","C":"Serosa blocks spread to the regional nodes","D":"Mucosal cancer cells stop dividing early","E":"No lymphatics in the lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colonic lamina propria normally has no lymphatic vessels, so cancer may grow within the epithelium and lamina propria before it reaches lymphatics at the muscularis mucosae. While a lesion is confined to the mucosa, endoscopic removal is adequate, pending microscopic review. The key barrier is absent lamina propria lymphatics, not the basal lamina, which the carcinoma has already crossed into lamina propria. The serosa is irrelevant here; mucosal lesions cannot reach nodes because lamina propria lacks lymphatics. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous adenomas have more malignant potential than tubular ones ('villous histology is villainous') (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the polyp stalk is continuous with the colonic submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The key barrier is absent lamina propria lymphatics, not the","C":"The serosa is irrelevant here; mucosal lesions cannot reach "}},{"id":"sdl24_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with ulcerative colitis has a colonic biopsy. Lymphatic vessels are numerous in the lamina propria between the glands. What does this finding indicate?","choices":{"A":"Active inflammation","B":"Healed remission phase","C":"Normal colonic mucosa","D":"Early adenoma growth","E":"Ischemic mucosal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lamina propria lymphatics are normally absent. In ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue brings new blood and lymphatic vessels, so more of them signals active inflammation, and their disappearance tracks response to treatment. In remission, lamina propria lymphatics disappear, so numerous vessels argue against healing. Normal colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatics between glands. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis complications include toxic megacolon and colorectal cancer, with risk rising in pancolitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: treatment progress in ulcerative colitis can be monitored by biopsies showing lymphatics disappearing (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"In remission, lamina propria lymphatics disappear, so numero","C":"Normal colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatics between gland"}},{"id":"sdl24_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has biopsies showing new lymphatic vessels forming in the lamina propria. Expression of which factor is linked to this lymphangiogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Transforming growth factor alpha","B":"Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met","C":"Vascular endothelial growth factor","D":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 signaling","E":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 axis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lymphangiogenesis in ulcerative colitis is linked to the expression of vascular endothelial growth factors (VEGFs). Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: in ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue forms with proliferating blood and lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a colonoscopy. Crescent-shaped folds project into the colonic lumen. What forms these folds?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal plicae circulares","B":"Lymphoid nodules in the mucosa","C":"Fat in the omental appendices","D":"Muscularis externa contraction","E":"Pressure from the luminal feces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The smooth colonic mucosa has no plicae or villi. Its semilunar folds are formed by contraction of the muscularis externa. Colonic lymphatic nodules distort gland spacing and extend into the submucosa rather than forming crescentic folds. Omental appendices are fatty projections of the outer serosa and do not project into the lumen. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra form because teniae bundles penetrate the circular layer, so colonic segments contract independently (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: mass peristaltic movements of the colon usually occur about once a day (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic lymphatic nodules distort gland spacing and extend i","C":"Omental appendices are fatty projections of the outer serosa"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a colon biopsy. Absorptive cells show dilated intercellular spaces. Which lateral membrane pump drives their fluid uptake?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","B":"H⁺/K⁺-ATPase","C":"Ca²⁺-ATPase","D":"Proton V-ATPase","E":"Cu²⁺-ATPase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells transport water and electrolytes with the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase system in their lateral membranes, so the intercellular space is often dilated. H⁺/K⁺-ATPase is the parietal cell proton pump of the stomach, not the colonic absorptive pump. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are morphologically like small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the dilated intercellular space reflects active fluid transport (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"H⁺/K⁺-ATPase is the parietal cell proton pump of the stomach"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has a research colon biopsy. After leaving the stem cell niche, how many more divisions do new colonic cells undergo?","choices":{"A":"Five to six","B":"Ten to twelve","C":"Two to three","D":"Twenty or more","E":"Thirty to forty"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Newly generated colonic cells undergo two or three more divisions as they migrate up toward the luminal surface. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive and goblet cells turn over in about 6 days (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic stem cells reside at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a right hemicolectomy. With which region's lymphoid tissue is his cecal GALT continuous?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Distal duodenum","D":"Upper jejunum","E":"Anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GALT of the large intestine is continuous with that of the terminal ileum and is more extensively developed. The jejunum lacks aggregated lymphatic nodules, which are characteristic of the ileum (Peyer patches). Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic lymphatic nodules can distort the regular spacing of the glands (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the appendix has many lymphatic nodules that extend into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The jejunum lacks aggregated lymphatic nodules, which are ch"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a sigmoid colectomy. Lymph leaves the network in the deepest layer of the colonic mucosa. Where does it go next?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal and muscular plexuses","B":"Lacteals in the lamina propria","C":"Vessels near the luminal surface","D":"Portal vein radicles in the liver","E":"Thoracic duct directly from mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"From the network in the muscularis mucosae, lymphatics drain to plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa, then to regional lymph nodes. The colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in its core near the luminal surface; small lymphatics are found only at the gland bases. Colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in its core near the luminal surface; small lymphatics are found only at the gland bases. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colonic lamina propria core between glands normally lacks lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymphatic endothelium can be labeled with the D2-40 antibody (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in it","C":"Colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in its co"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old man's colon mucosa is imaged by scanning EM. Clefts divide the surface into territories. About how many gland openings does each hold?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2","B":"3 to 5","C":"5 to 10","D":"500 to 1000","E":"25 to 100"},"correct":"E","explanation":"On scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories that each contain 25 to 100 gland openings. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.29). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic glands are straight tubular glands that extend through the full thickness of the mucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colonic mucosa lacks villi and plicae circulares, so its luminal surface is flat (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"}]};
